      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>.....- ...r.+K• . ... 00, ..-:. – , .„*. . ~. . :::: ...... .... .......--...</head>
        <p>.....- ...r.+K• . ... 00, ..-:. - , .„*. . ~. . :::: ...... .... .......--...</p>
        <p>Female War Labour.—This Week has been introduced into the Lymm district female war labour—a lady being installed in the railway booking office at Heatley.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Drill.—A Battalion Drill of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment—including the Lymm section—is to take place at Rostherne on Monday. More Belgians Leave.—Yesterday afternoon another batch of Belgian refugees bade good- bye to " Beechwood "—their Lymm; home. The reason is not stated.</p>
        <p>"Badly Cut Up In Charge."—Writing home on the 10th, a Lymm " Terrier " said: "One of our companies Made a charge on Friday Morning. They got badly cut up, and lost very nearly all the officers. . ."</p>
        <p>• Church Parade At F ront —Private Jack • Moston, of the "Terriers," writes from the Front: We are having lovely weather; we have just been to a church parade and heard a fine sermon. .. . I wish I was coming home for Whit Sunday; it would be nice. . . ."</p>
        <p>Soldier's Sad Leave.—Sergeant Robert Jones— who, a few weeks ago, was over at Lymm on sick leave from Sevenoaks—has. just had the misfortune to lose his youngest child, aged four months. To enable him to attentl the funeral, the sergeant was granted 48 hours' leave of absence.</p>
        <p>"Master Thurston" Cake At Front —Writing to his wife early in the week Sergeant Clare says: "I have received a parcel from the Rev. G. B. Thurston, containing some of Master John's christening cake, and have given all our men a little, and all were very delighted with it. It was very nice."</p>
        <p>Butchers' Hours.—At the Spread Eagle Hotel on Tuesday evening, the Lymm butchers met to consider the curtailment—or otherwise—of their shop hours during the present crisis. After much discussion they adjourned their decision until a meeting held last night. The result appears in o-ir "Local Intelligence," page 5.</p>
        <p>"Terriers" Entertain Friends.—Private Jas. Gilbert writes: "We had Peter Percival on our ground the other day—having tea with us —and he does look well. We had another man, named Wibberley, from Statham. . , . Peter is doing well, and he would have liked to have stated with us for good; but they would not let him V'</p>
        <p>Did Their Duty.—At the Lymm Petty Ses- sions, held at the Police Station on Wednesday morning, there were on the Bench: Messrs. A. Watkin (in the chair), S. Davies, R. H. Goto- bed, and Holden; and the Hon. J. E. Cross.— Mr. Gotobed, having just been elected to the Council chairmanship, was duly sworn in as a magistrate.</p>
        <p>Ii 1</p>
        <p>Promoted Corporal.—Private George Drink- water, of the Bth South Lancashires—whose home is at Rush Green, Lymm, and who is now training in the South—has just been Pro- moted to the rank of Corporal. He also, by the way, has two brothers in the South Lanca- shires.</p>
        <p>?, 4.1 1• '</p>
        <p>Cookery Mistress' Appointment.—Miss Mary Harrison, cookery mistress at Lymm under the Cheshire Education Committee---which position she has held for a year or two with all-round satisfaction—has been appointed to a similar position at Hinley, near Atherton. Yesterday Miss Harrison left Lymm to take up her new duties.</p>
        <p>Where Has The Cake Gone?—Private P. Percival writes to 3, West Hyde, New&amp;lds, to thank his wife for a parcel: "I got your par- cel last night, but there was no cake in it [as had been sent]. There was some tobacco and matches in it, and somebody else's parcel tied up in it!" It is rather a pity about the cake, for it was a capital piece of confectionery— and the postage, too, was a trifle heavy. The private has, however, received safely parcels from one or two other relatives.</p>
        <p>J:1</p>
        <p>One Man: Two Offences.—Thomas Coppock, of Chapel-street, Stockport, was summoned at Wednesday's Sessions for having, on the high- way_ at Lymm on Friday, May 7th, driven a motor cycle and side-car without a rear light; and also for failing to produce his licence when requested by a constable.—To both offences he pleaded guilty.—A constable said, that at 10.30 p.m. on Penny Hill he whistled to the driver, who stopped his machine. He said "the rear light had been a proper nuisance. It was connected with the front lamp, and seemed to go low, and it would jog out." Asked for his licence he said he had one, but then 'found he had left it in another suit when changing, previous to leaving home. He sent it on, however, three lays later.—Defendant said a Lancashire constable told him the rear light bye-law was not operative in Cheshire.— The Chairman: Well, at any rate the light was' out. You will be fined half-a-crown, in- cluding costs, in each case.</p>
        <p>n</p>
        <p>Handicapped School Attendance.—John Hil- ton, labourer, of The Green, Partington, appeared at Wednesday's Lymm Petty Sessions in respect of his daughter Annie (13) not attending Cadishead School.—Mr. C. E. Hill (attendance officer for Sale district) said the girl, having passed Standard V., had made five attendances out of a possible 115 during 13 weeks. She came under the Cheshire bye- laws. In the previous year she attended four times from September 16th.—It transpired that the father- had been ill, whitst the mother, a cripple, needed her daughter's help at home. The earnings of the girl's brother and sister were needed to help to keep the house, and so the latter could not stay at home in Annie's stead.—The father said he did not know he would have to apply to the committee. He could not afford to pay for domestic help.— The Chairman (Mr. A. Watkin) said defendant was taking advantage of the committee's pre- vious leniency in similar cases, and he would be fined half-a-crown, including costs. If the girl did not attend regularly in future the man would be severely punished.</p>
        <p>Why Child Did Not Attend.—Edward Black- burn, of Moss-brow, Warburton, was sum- moned at Wednesday's Petty Sessions in re- spect of his son Stanley (51) not yet attending school.—He said the boy's health was not good, and then when he had been going to send him sickness broke out at the Partington School, and "he thought he had better keep the boy away for a bit."-The Chairman: And then you took the law into your own hands!— Mr. Mercer (registrar): This man seems set on defiance, and in spite of warnings sent out by the Chairman and the Clerk is determined not to send the child to school. We ask you to assist us in these matters. The case has been adjourned four times.—Defendant: I am on Government work, and my wife was not fit to attend; she has not been well all this year. I leave home at 6.30 a.m.•and don't get back till 10 at night—The Chairman: But you ,have only two children. Surely you could send him. You are. a .very foolish man; why didn't you appear before the committeeP—Defendant : I have only seen one letter.—Mr. Mercer: Well, I have sent the letters addressed to him.—ln fining the man 2s. 6d., including costs, the Chairman said that the next time the case came up he would be severely dealt with.</p>
        <p>LIYMM</p>
        <p>"Two Manchester Girls."—ln our last issue we gave a _poem on "The Death of the First Lymm Warrior"—written by Mr. James H. Appleton (17), of Statham. This week he has received a letter which says: " . . . We</p>
        <p>wish to congratulate you on your splendid poem, which we found extremely interesting to read in 'The Warrington Examiner.' We are two Manchester girls, but we hdve many friends in Warrington, so have 'The Examiner' forwarded on to us every week by one of our friends. . . . Sorry to hear of your chum (Private Leigh) being killed in action; but . . . he gave his life for his King and country. . . . If it should be anyone's luck to capture Kaiser Wil- liam, we hope it will be the Russians—because if the British got hold of him he would have an easier life of it. . . .—We remain, yours *lily, Sybil Worthington and Florence Field- ing."—Mr. Appleton has written another capital poem on "The Loss of the Lusitania."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>POOR RATE INCREASE.</head>
        <p>POOR RATE INCREASE.</p>
        <p>Sanctioned By The Justices.</p>
        <p>At the Police Court on Wednesday, the justices sanctioned the levying of a poor rate of is. Bd. in the 5.; on buildings and 10d. in the £ on land: Last year's rates were is. 6d. and 9d. respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MOTOR ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>MOTOR ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Three Lymm People Involved At</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>When Mr. Edward Gilberts, of " Newfield "- was coming from his work on Wednesday night at about a quarter to- six, and was proceeding along Thelwall-lane, Warrington, the motor- cars of Mr. Marsh (of Bradley Hall, Cherry- lane, Lymm) and Mr. J. Houghton (of West- lea, Lymm), approached in the opposite direc- tions and attempted to pass each other. At that particular spot the road is narrow. Mr. Gilberts was knocked down and fell, striking his head on the ground. For a short time rendered unconscious, he was carried into an adjacent cottage, where he was attended to. He was then conveyed home in one of the motors, and is now progressing well—under the care of Dr. Be Jong. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>FIRE AT STATHAM.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT STATHAM.</p>
        <p>Considerable Damage Covered By</p>
        <p>Insurance.</p>
        <p>At "The Croft," Statham—the residence of Mr. Jones-Parry—a fire broke out at 2.30 on Wednesday afternoon. The outbreak occurred near the roof, where some painters were engaged on the outside of the facie spouting. It is, of course, not known who caused it. The fire bell was rung at the Lymm Station ten minutes later, and the brigade turned out under the superintendence of Mr. Wm. Kirk- patrick. On arrival at the house, they found the roof on fire; one of the attic bedrooms, with its furniture and contents, was also ablaze. The flames were findlly extinguished after six busy and exciting hour, and by order of Mr. Melbourne (agent for the property), the fire- fighters cleared all the broken slates, Sze., from the roof and the floors.</p>
        <p>Before their departure Messrs. Melbourne and Jones-Parry complimented the brigade on their smart work.</p>
        <p>The Damage. The damage is estimated at between £5O and £6O. It is covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>"RAID ON GERMAN."</head>
        <p>"RAID ON GERMAN."</p>
        <p>Rumoured Event And Police Court</p>
        <p>Sequel.</p>
        <p>John Blackledge, jun., whose address was that of a farm at Agden, was summoned at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday for annoying street passengers by the use of obscene language at Lymm on Saturday night.—He pleaded guilty. Sergeant Davies said that at 10.30 p.m. he was in Grammar School-lane, when Blackledge came up and addressed a group of young men who were there—using obscene language. He (the sergeant) went across and asked him why, and defendant at first denied the offence.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Sutherland: Is it a fact that on this night the house of a naturalised Ger- man was to be attacked?—The Sergeant: Yes; I was there in consequence. Defendant had nothing to say except that he was not drunk.</p>
        <p>The Sergeaut: He had had a little, I should say!</p>
        <p>"There For That Purpose."</p>
        <p>The Superintendent: It is really a serious matter. The rumour had got about that this house was going to be attacked, and there is no doubt that this man and some others were there for that purpose; but we were there before them. .</p>
        <p>Defendant: I didn't go up tb.ere with any intention of that kind at all.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Sutherland said Blackledge had not been before the Court before.</p>
        <p>"If You Want To Show Your Feelings-" The Chairman (Mr. A. Watkin) to defendant : If you want to show your feelings towards the Germans you had better join the Army. You seem to be the right sort of man for it. It is very fine to come here and talk about Ger- mans in this way, but you had better go and fight them. Defendant was fined 55., including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>TIRED TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>TIRED TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>Feeling A Bit Worn-Out.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning Mrs. G. Davies received a letter from her son, Private Percy Clare: "It is rumoured around here that we are going to have a rest. . . . There is not one of us but feels run down. We always feel tired—what with the hard work at night and when we are supposed to be haring our few days' rest from the trenches, and when we are in the trenches, and with all the noise of the battlefield I can tell you we do feel like a good rest. We have had rather a stiff three months; but never mind! . . . We keep on going, for it can't go on for ever. . . . On the 10th he wrote: "We are at present in the midst of heavy fighting, and are con- tinually in the trenches. This makes the eleventh day 1n the fire-trench and supports. ." In word dispatched on the 12th, he said:— "About three o'clock this morning we arrived at the fields from the trenches. . . .</p>
        <p>Harry Cadman has got back to us again. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>FENNINGS'</head>
        <p>FENNINGS'</p>
        <p>THE GREAT PREVENTIVE &amp; CURE OF FEVERS &amp; INFECTIOUS OEMS. SOLD SORE THROAT r4' • r.r -3,••• EVERYWHERE ~" • WITH ONE DOSE. with ,ER;</p>
        <p>CURED</p>
