      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>Tith EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 22, 1915.</head>
        <p>Tith EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 22, 1915.</p>
        <p>LYIVIM TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>MORE KILLED AND WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>The Lymm "Terriers" at the Front came out of the trenches on Tuesday, May 11th, after serving in them for ten days. The weather was very good. Those ten days were rather xciting, and the Lymm men sustained the following casualties:</p>
        <p>KILLED. Captain B. S. Walker (Southport)</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Private Stanley Gibbs. Private Cecil Acton (High Legh)</p>
        <p>Private J. Clare writes: "Cecil -Act9n and S. Gibbs have got wounded, but not serious." Considerable anxiety has arisen in the/ cases :</p>
        <p>-- • Private Henry Brookes and Private H. Cadman (previously wounded), who are reported wounded by the War Office— for no other news of these cases has reached Lymm! Others of the Lymm "Terriers" have served different periods with different sections. Sergeant Harold Clare has been at the base hospital for a day or two, suffering from neu- ralgia, but by now will probably be back in the firing-line. Writing to his wife a day or two ago he said he wished to be back with thel "lads." This latest periett in the trenches is described by Private P. Percival: "We have had a rough do' of 4, I can tell you. I dare say you will see about it in the papers. It is awful—that gas they are using. We saw fellows come staggering along as if they were drunk; and men lying all over the place, waiting to be buried. But lam all right so far. Our battalion has been in the trenches for over, a three-week, and now we have come out for a few days' rest. . ." Yesterday morning a note from Private H. Cadman, dated May 15th, ran: "Our com- pany had rather a long stay in the trenches this last time, for they have been in the sup- ports and in the trenches for abolit sixteen or seventeen days. .. ." Private J. Read's communication of the 11th, ran: "I expect we are being relieved to-night from the trenches—after being four days in the firing line. We shall be glad of a short rest, I can tell you Just before being Wounded, Private S. Gibbs wrote: "Some of the other reinforcements have joined us, but not very many. Up here on top of the Hill we have a fine view of the German trenches, and we can see them at work. This is the first time I have seen a live German. We have seen plenty of dead through glasses." Writing five days after this, Private James Gilbert said: "We are hoping to have another day or two out yet." On Saturday, May 15th—the latest news—the men were still " resting " out of the trenches. PriVate Henry Cadman, writing during a respite after two. days in the trenches, , says: "We are now having a rest, and the town which we were in when I left them is an awful sight now. The Germans are shelling every jay with 17in. shells, and I can tell you they have made it a heap of ruins; it is blazing with fire at night. We had used to be billeted in the town, but now we have to go and live in the fields—or anywhere we can get. I am sure there must be heaps of dead in the place; and no one has to go anywhere nearer the town,' for the shells are dropping all round the place., . _</p>
        <p>- Lance-Corporal S. Linley and Private Arthur Hadden, having been home on a few days' fur- lough after being wounded and accidentally hurt respectively, have this week re-joined an- other section of their regiment in a Cheshire town.</p>
        <p>The former is hoping to visit home again to- day, to stay the weck-entl. Lance-Corporal Edwin Blease (wounded) ar- rived home on Friday evening, returning to his depot on Thursday afternoon. Private James Taylor—who was the first Lymm "Terrier" to be wounded—remains convalescent in the Grantham Red Cross Hos- pital. His left /arm is still useless; and in a despondent letter home he remarks: "I don't think I shall ever get back the use of my arm."</p>
        <p>Further silly rumours from Lymm have reached the " lads " in the trenches. Private Henry Brookes writes: "There is not the slightest bit of truth about Joe Brazendale being killed. Where such rumours get afloat I don't know. It is very curious. . ..."</p>
        <p>Yesterday Private Percy Clare wrote: "The weather has broken over here. We are having some jery wet weather, and it is not so nice living in fields. . . . Everybody is up to the boot tops in mud. But we can put up with it"</p>
        <p>this job was over, as we are all badly wanting a rest. . ." This word arrived on Monday. Private Brookes' wife is staying at South- port. On hearing the news site was greatly upset; but wrote: "I have had a .letter this morning from Harry. It was written on Fri4lay, so_l think there must be some mistake —and it must be some other soldier of the same name. Harry once told me there were several with the name of Brookes in their battalion."</p>
        <p>PRIVATE S. GIBBS. Wounded.</p>
        <p>shall post this when I get to Southampton. I don't know where and to which hospital in England lam going. I will let you know later. Don't worry. . . ." Officer's Tribute.</p>
        <p>Svond-Lieutenant T. B. Morgan writes on May 13th:—" Dear Mrs. Gibbs,—Just a line to say how sorry I am that your husband has been wounded in the left hand. It is not at all serious. He has done some excellent work [as a range-finder] in locating stores, guns, tkc., be- hind the German lines."</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare—who has been acting at the Front as Private Gibbs' reserve, and now succeeds him jn the duties, and who subscribes himself as "Stan's pal," also drops a line to the wife of the wounded private: "Stanley has got wounded; but don't upset yourself, as he is quite all right. . . . I left him last night [the 12th] and he was in the best of spirits. He told me that he was right for what we call blighty '—that means England; so I hope it will not be long before he gets home. lam sorry to lose him, as we have ben together all along. . . ."</p>
        <p>.. :.:„. .-iiif:jtot‘i.•l'.;:!:,,fighip.:).:i:',2.'!....,,,,,,::::::,..,..',..':':',Vi.;:.*letittkikii* -tit.„*...:14._!:""..:-.441e':.,,,f.0.,.. ~. ~...„..,...,.....,:,....„..:1y...;:r..4..`,,?:;.'....:.... ~:5.,,,-:,:,.,',Rv...;5.4.4,.A!..:•::::..,...,.:,,..,:..". ;.f0.05k.";.,:;:3;tkiii,4.;.;i::::.i.L ~ . ;:.:i..4,-.7..;;...:.,,,'..•...f: ' ''''s' . N-. ' , ::` . 4 0*.1.:.. t ..,.., ~ ....„ . ..... ... , ...:.. : , . ... . .. ...-- ~ . ~,,5t,....:'.:•„.'....,•-, - • , • p :;:,,,,,,,:.,:::;,....:-: . • :.."...„,....:::,...-:::.: ~ :...:,,,.:,' ' - ' .. .. • • ....'. ~ , , ;:i.r:'''''''''''-''''''...;.:, : '..:: . S. '. ,' '.... . ' •'' *At # 0 . . .....:.,.. .. ~ -,...„ .-. ~,.,. .:.: ~. . . .....,,.,..„-...., ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>CAPTAIN B. S. WALKER.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN B. S. WALKER.</p>
        <p>"'Terriers'' Very Good Friend" Killed By Sniper.</p>
        <p>. It is with deep regret that we announce the death of Captain B. S. Walker, of the sth Cheshires—who has been shot in the firing-line by a Thiper. Only three weeks ago he had been promoted from the rank of lieutenant. The Captain was not a Lymm man; his home is at Southport. Along with Captain Hartley, he was, however, in charge of the „local "Terriers "4,-by whom he was most deeply re- spected and honoured. From these sad soldiers many magnificent tributes come to hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>" No One Could Realise It."</head>
        <p>" No One Could Realise It."</p>
        <p>Private S. Gibbs (who has himself been wounded) writes: "I am sorry to say that our officer, Lieutenant B. S. Walker, was killed by a sniper. Not five minutes before it happened he paid me a visit. He and another officer had beem on the top of Hill 60 all day observing. The last words the officer said to me were 'Good news, Gibbs, keep your head down, and good luck!'</p>
        <p>- "The next thing a sergeant came to the bottom of the hill to say that the officer had been killed. No one in the trenches could realise it, as he was one of the best of officers, and every man liked him. We have lost a very good friend. Captain Hartley is very upset."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>" Our Dear Platoon Officer."</head>
        <p>" Our Dear Platoon Officer."</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Clare's word home runs : "I am very sorry to say that we have lost our dear platoon officer, Captain Walker. . . It upset us very much, because he was one that we could follow anywhere; and if he had ever said: 'Come on over the trench and at 'ern!' we should have gone. He was .really a very good and featless officer; but it must have been God's will. ."</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Hadden says: "He was a great favourite amongst the Lymm lads, and will be missed very much...." Private Percy Clare says: "We have sus- tained a great loss by the death of our officer. He was a good, brave man, and . . . a gen- tleman. He was sniped. . . . It has cast a deep gloom all over our company."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>"A Nice Funeral."</head>
        <p>"A Nice Funeral."</p>
        <p>"He looked after his lads as though we were his own brothers. We have put him to rest this afternoon [the 12th]. I was one of the carriers. It was a nice funeral; all the Bat- talion lined up in the field, and we marched between them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>PRIVATE HENRY BROOKES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HENRY BROOKES.</p>
        <p>AVar. Office Intimation Of Vomits</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>Alone Received.</head>
        <p>Alone Received.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Taylor, of 1, West Hyde, Newfield," received on Sunday Morning notification from the War Office—via the Shrewsbury depot—that her son, Private Henry Brookes (a: stretcher- bearer with the Lymm Territorials at the Front) was in the Boulogne base hospital suffer- ing from powder-burns to the face and left hand. The notification was dated May I.3th, and the Private's correct name, number and address were given.</p>
        <p>—By a mistake the fleliyery of this letter had been delayed for two clays. '</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother, however, rin May 13th, from the trenches, Private Brookes made no mention of being wounded. "We have just left the trenche;s for a time," he said— "perhaps four or five days. . . . Well, I wish</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>PRIVATE STANLEY GIBBS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE STANLEY GIBBS.</p>
        <p>In Coast Hospital, Wounded In Left</p>
        <p>Arm.</p>
        <p>The first news of Private Stanley Gibbs hav- ing beeii wounded to reach Lymm, arrived on Saturday - morning—whbn Mrs. Gibbs, a Henry-street, received the following word from Private J. Read '(another Lymm com- rade), written ta, the 11th : Dear Mrs. Gib am writing to inform you of your husband, Stanley, being wounded to-day in his left hand by a bullet. He has been admitted into hospital, and is going on well. He will soon be coming to see you at Lymm, I hope. Your husband asked me if I would write to you. I must also inform you of another Lymm chap getting wounded on the same date, Cecil Acton, who was shot through the shoulder by a bullet. He is going on very well. They have both gone into .hospital.</p>
        <p>The wounded Private himself wrote the news as an addition to a letter he had written to his wife just before the casualty:—" This letter was written just as I got wounded. I am well. I got shot through the left arm. I am in a hospital train going to Boulogne. I hope to be in England soon." On a card dated the 14th, and written at Rouen—en route for the homeland—Private Gibbs stated: "I am leaving here to-night, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Visited By His Wife.</head>
        <p>Visited By His Wife.</p>
        <p>The wounded " Terrier " now lies, satis- factorily progressing, at the Liverpool 112rs- pital ; and on Wednesday he was viSited by his wife, who found him most cheerful and con- tented.</p>
        <p>The bullet has passed through the palm of his left hand—fracturing two fingers and the knuckle of his middle finger. He says he will probably be eight weeks recovering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>His Career.</head>
        <p>His Career.</p>
        <p>Born twenty-three years ago, Private Gibbs is a native of Aden. Leaving the boiungton School at the age of 14, he went to work as a wheelwright for Mr. Clarke—with whom he remained for two or three years. On similar work he then joined the firm of Messrs. Hampton, az Thelwall; but latterly had been in the employ of Mr. W. Kirkpatrick, Bridgewater-street, Lymm. He had belonged to the Territorials for five years, and had attended their several camps. At the outbreak of hostilities he was called up, and, after training in different parts of the country, went in February to the Front as a "range-finder." His letters home "The Examiner" has from time to time published. He is a worshipper at Lymm Church. Before his marriage he was prominently connected with the church at Oughtringtoh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>PRIVATE CECIL ACTON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE CECIL ACTON.</p>
        <p>High Legh Man Slightly Wounded In</p>
        <p>Shoulder.</p>
        <p>Mrs. S. Gibbs was the first person in Lymm to hear that Private Cecil Acton had been slightly wounded in the shoulder whilst at the Front with the sth Cheshires. She learned it</p>
        <p>PRIVATE CECIL ACTON</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>through the medium of a letter from Private Jack Read, which is given above.</p>
        <p>A Native of Crowley.</p>
        <p>Aged 22 and unmarried, the gallant Private was born at Crowley, near Appleton; but he has for many years made his home at, High Legh. In times of peace he is employed as a gardener, at Dean's Green, by Colonel Gibves. He joined the Terri4urial Forces abcst three years ago, and had attended all the annual camps before the outbreak of war. Then he was called up with the other Lymm "lads," and with them went out to the war zone after the customary homeland training.</p>
        <p>The Private belong to the Church of England</p>
        <p>DON'T MOE OLD.—Keep the ravages of time at bay bY darkening the grey streaks with LOCETER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER, which deepens to a natural colour in a few days. Lockyer's maintains a youthful appearance. Large bot's. everywhere. Post free 1;6 Bedford Laboratory; London, 9.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</p>
        <p>AVounded Again Two Days Wei.</p>
        <p>Return To 'Trenches?</p>
        <p>Mrs. H. Cadman, of Booth's Hill, has received from the War Office the following notification of her ,stepson's second woundffig</p>
        <p>He had been wounded in the ehest. His Cor- rect age also was given. Last week; as we have previously stated, Private Cadman had recovered from his first wounds, and returned to the trenches. Having been there with his Lymm comrades for two days, he wrote to his sister, Mrs. G. Rixon, of Pepper-street : "I am in the best of health, and glad to say that I am better and have joined my regiment again. I .have done two days in the trenches— which the Company have been in for sixteen or seventeen days. We are now having a rest." This letter WitA written on the 12th.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning- Mrs. Rixon received further word, written by the Private, on the 15th: "I am in the best of health. . . I have not had a letter since I got wounded. [Several have been sent—every week]. . . . They did not find me. . . . lam just going on guard. . . ." Than this no further report of the wounding —except rumour—has reached the township.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>F. 11 I. NS AS GOOD SOLDIERS AS ENGLISH.</head>
        <p>F. 11 I. NS AS GOOD SOLDIERS AS ENGLISH.</p>
        <p>"As for the war ending soon," writes Private James Gilbert, of the sth Cheshires at the Front, to his friend, Mr. Fred Vernon (who also lives at Newfield, Lymm), "it never will; and if they do not come to tertns they will never finish it under two years or more. For nobody knows what it is like, only those that are over here—and they can hardly tell! "They used to tell us in England that the Germans could not shoot; but can't they! They are quite as good as us in everything from what I canesee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>"THE MAJOR FELL SICK."</head>
        <p>"THE MAJOR FELL SICK."</p>
        <p>mna "Terrier's" Happy Stay In Pretty French Village.</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Had'den (C Company sth Cheshires) writes from the Front to his motker at Pepper-street:— "You will be surprised to hear that I have been away from the regiment for about seven or eight days. The Major fell sick, so I went down the•line with him. We stayed at a big house In a very pretty French village. We had glorious weather with brilliant sunshine, and the country around was quite a picture—and so quiet and peaceful it did so remind me of home!</p>
        <p>"It came to an end yesterday. He was ordered farther down the line, and officers' servants can only go a certain distance with them. So I had to return to the company. I am once again with the Lymm lads. I expect the next time they go in the trenches I shall be with them; so it will be a little more ex- perience for me. Things are a lithe quieter at present. . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>SCOTS GUARD'S LUCK.</head>
        <p>SCOTS GUARD'S LUCK.</p>
        <p>Private B. Elliott's Two Narrow "Shaves" In Big Battle.</p>
        <p>Private Ben Elliott, of the Scots Guards— who lives with his parents at. Canterbury Cot- tage, Lymm—writes a graphic letter to his sister, Miss Millie Elliott, at Altrincham. . . . Since I wrote you last we have been in a big battle, and I have had two very narrow shaves. . . . As we were coming out of the trenches a bullet went through my pack (on my back). cornerways. There is a hole right through, but I don't mind. Then on Saturday I was out in my dug-out when I felt something hit my leg, and it was a piece of shrapnel from a shell that burst in front of our trench.</p>
        <p>"I have got that. . . . I have got quite a lot of little relics like that, and shall stick to them. . . . A tale is attached to every one. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>So Near and Yet so Far.</head>
        <p>So Near and Yet so Far.</p>
        <p>" You have no idea what we boys are going through, but I trust to God to give us courage.</p>
        <p>. . : Since I came out I have /Teen in Flan- ders, and Will [his brother] is in this battle. . . . I cannot come across him, but have no doubt I shall find him soon. . . . They are about two miles behind the trenches, and have a better chance than us, as they only have to watch where the shells are bursting, and have sa chance of getting out of the way. We have the bullets and shell-fire to watch--so that we are bobbing up and down all the time. That is why we have to keep coming ouf of the trenches—to get our nerves cool again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Eating " That " Cake.</head>
        <p>Eating " That " Cake.</p>
        <p>"Well, I think that cake is lovely, and you would have thought I had never seen a cake in my life if you had seen me sitting in a big field at three o'clock this morning eating it, with shells bursting all about me. I just kept bobbing my head up and down. . . ,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>MISSING AIRMAN.</head>
        <p>MISSING AIRMAN.</p>
        <p>News Received That He Is A Prisoner.</p>
        <p>Flight Sub-Lieutenant S. Osborne Grove, of Dean's Green, Lymm, has been reported miss- ing—as we stated last week.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning a War Office telegram informed his mother that he is now a prisoner of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>BATCH OF WOUNDED</head>
        <p>BATCH OF WOUNDED</p>
        <p>Arrive At "Brookfield "In Fine Spirits.</p>
        <p>The hatch of wounded warriors—whose arrival at "Brookfield," the Lymm Red Cross Hospi- tal, has been expected for a week or two, as we have before mentioned—" landed" at that in- stitution on Wednesday afternoon. _</p>
        <p>They occupy all the Wards and the operating- room—which have been beautifully re-decorated —including the new wards. Apart from their wounds, they appear in fine health, and their spirits are first rate. They progress satisfactorily under the care of Sister Colburn (the matron) and her well-trained staff of nurses.</p>
        <p>The new arrivals number eighteen, so that there are now twenty-four wounded men in ",Brookfield:" The new-comers include: Gunner Arnold, Royal Field Artillery. Private W. Sutton, Black Watch. Private George Beattie, 2nd Black Watch. Private Wm. Birtwistle, King's Own Royal Lancasters. Private Foley, 2nd Munsters. Private S. Derry, 2nd Lancashire Fusiliers. Private Wm. Grand, Ist Northamptons. Lance-Corporal Dodson, Anglesey Royal Engi- neers.. Private Howard, Ist Sherwood Foresters. Private John Gorst, Ist Loyal North Lama- shires. Private Wm. Roffie, 2nd Lincolnshires. Private James Robertson, let Argyle Suther- land Highlanders. Private Melville, Ist Black Watch. Private McGillicuddy, 2nd Munsters. Rifleman John May, 2nd Rifle 'Brigade. And three others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>Poor Father.</head>
        <p>Poor Father.</p>
        <p>Over the shop door of a pork butcher in a village in the Eastern .counties may be seen a signboard representing a man in a black coat, brandishing a hatchet, with the inscription: "John Smith kills pigs like his father."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>RURAL ROUTINE.</head>
        <p>RURAL ROUTINE.</p>
        <p>Runcorn R.D.C.'s Lighting Arrange- ments With Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>REPAIRING 'HIE ROADS.</head>
        <p>REPAIRING 'HIE ROADS.</p>
        <p>In the absence of the Chairman (Mr. Angus) owing to the illness of his wife, the Vice-Chairman (Mr. W. Harrison) presided at the monthly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council, at Dutton, on Tuesday. Before taking his seat, the Vice-chairman proposed that a vote of sympathy be passed. The suggestion was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>NEW LIGHTING RATES PENDING.</head>
        <p>NEW LIGHTING RATES PENDING.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Council wrote giving twelve months' notice to terminate the agree- ments for the lighting of Walton Inferior, Latchford Without, ra ppc nhall, Walton Superior and Appleton. The remark was passed that it was to be presumed the Corporation meant to raise the prices. The Clerk: Yes.</p>
        <p>It was observed that the Corporation were only lighting the different places for a few hours nightly, and only on alternate nights in some instances. The question was asked whether the Council were going to get anything back.</p>
        <p>The Clerk replied that reductions were being made.</p>
        <p>RING OUT THE NEW: RING IN THE OLD. Mr. Pilling rose to move a resolution iii regard to the future meetings of the Council. He thought a great mistake was made by the Council when they decided to hold the meet- ings monthly instead of fortnightly as origin- ally. They met for business every fortnight, and he saw no reason why a general meeting should not be held every fortnight. In fact,* there was every reason why they should revert to the old system. It would expedite business considerably. He moved that the change be made. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward seconded. lie was perfectly satisfied that he was wrong when he thought that the system of meeting monthly would be an improvement, and he now felt that the best thing, the Council could do was to revert to the hitherto prevailing custom. The proposition was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>REPRESENTATION RUSE.</head>
        <p>A REPRESENTATION RUSE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Comity Education Com- mittee wrote stating that t hat authority had agreed to the inclusion of Mr. Jas. Spruce in their number, as representative of the . Rural Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling proposed that Mr. Spruce be appointed by the Council their representative on the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, however, pointed out that if the Council elected Mr. Spruce their repre- sentative, the area would lose in representa- tion. Mr. Spruce was already an independent member, and if the Council appointed another of their number they would virtually have two representatives on the committee.</p>
        <p>Ur. Spruce said he thought it would be better for 'the Council to appoint someone else.</p>
        <p>and made a_proposition to -that-effect.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Woodward seconded, and the Council concurred and appointed Mr. Baxter,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>HIGHWAY ESTIMATES.</head>
        <p>THE HIGHWAY ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>The highway estimates were presented. The full amount it is proposed to expend in the various parishes during the year was £5,061. The amounts were allocated as follows: Acton Grange £25, Alvanley .E 97 Antrobus £245, Appleton £427, Aston £lO9, Aston Grange £lB, Bartington .29, Budworth £l3O, Clifton £96, Crowley £B5, Daresbury £2l. Dutton £B4, Frod- sham £315, Frodsham Lordship £386, Grappen- hall £177, Halton £BO, Hatton £l3B. Ilelsby £132, Keckwich £32, Kingsley £345, Kingswood £l3l, Latchford Without £lOB, Manley £ll6, Moore £45, Newton-by-lThresbury £lll, Mosley £257, Norton £5B, Preston-&amp;-thi-Hill £95, Sevenoaks £l2l, Stockham £35, Stockton Heath £152, Stretton £3O, Sutton £125, Thelwall £165, Walton Inferior £4B, Walton Superior £75. Weston .2177, Whitley Inferior £43, Whitley Superior £lOB.</p>
        <p>The estimates were referred to the Highways Committee.</p>
        <p>DR. ADAMS RETURNING.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that Dr. Adams-had giro him to understand that he would be back and ready for duty by the end of that week.</p>
        <p>HOSPITAL VAN DISINFECTION.</p>
        <p>The Halton Parochial Committee reported that Mr. Thomas Wright had been appointed chairman and Mr. J. B. Houghton vice-chair- man. It was recommended that the plans of ten cottages for Mr. T. Goanlden be rejected for non-compliance with the bylaws. Mr. .T. Booth, nightsoil contractor, wrote asking for a rise in his contract price owing to the increased cost of labour, and it was resolved that the price be increased to 4s. per house per annum during the war. It was resolved to ask the Rural District Council to request the Medical Superintendent of the hospital to report as to the method of disinfecting the hospital van.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>•QUESTION OF SANITATION.</head>
        <p>A •QUESTION OF SANITATION.</p>
        <p>The Weston Parochial Committee reported that Mr. J. B. Chadwick had been appointed chairman and Mr. J. Tudor vice-chairman. It was resolved that the notice of the Castner- Kellner Company be called to the sanitary con- ditions' existing at Sweetman's Buildings, and that the company be requested to give the matter immediate attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>ANTHRAX INFECTION.</head>
        <p>ANTHRAX INFECTION.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote in the matter of the recent case of anthrax, asking the Council to instruct their medical officer to prepare a report containing detailed informa- tion of the circumstances and measures taken to prevent the spread of the disease.</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING EXHIBITION. The National Road Conference Committee</p>
      </div>
