      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Dominant Position</head>
        <p>A Dominant Position</p>
        <p>:rttrrs to the otator</p>
        <p>COST 01."I'llE MUDGE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l saw in "The Examiner" that gr.-</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst said "that many state- ments had been made with regard to the prob- able cost of the bridge, and not a few of them. were extremely wide of the mark "; and that the actual cost was £19,962 19s. 10d. This• amount he also refers to as the total cost. Is not the wortily Alderman in his total cost very far from the mark himself? Surely such items as purchase of extra land and property. alteration of tram lines, electric and gas mains, paving and other necessary work must all be included in the real price the ratepayers have to pay for the bridge. The conundrum still is, what has it actually cost? Can anybody tell? A RATEPAYER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>RESTRICTIONS ON FEMALE</head>
        <p>RESTRICTIONS ON FEMALE</p>
        <p>L 11301.11.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the meeting held at the Town Hall last night, certain traders were asked to con- sider the question of a rearrangement of their staffs, with a view to estimating what men could be spared for war purposes. The chief suggestion made was that, wherever possible, female labour might be substituted. I tied to point out that, to help forward this arrange- ment, traders should have some assurance that they would not be interfered with unduly bY inspectors accustomed to a maximum use of red tape. Not being accustomed to speaking in public, I did not make my point understood. Aly reason for mentioning the matter at all as our experience at the small factory with which lam connected. We have, as far as possible, substituted girl labour on work- formerly done by youths. In doing so, we. worked two sets of girls, one set starting at, 6 a.m. and finishing at 1 p.m., and the second set starting at 1 p.m. and working until 8 p.lO. By this arrangement we were just about able to get as good an output as when we had youths working from 6 a.m. to 5.30 p.m.; the girls hod nice, easy hours (about 37 a week); we era- ployed more girls; and we were releasing eligibles for the Army or for Government work.. The Factory Inspector, however, made us droP the two shifts method. The result was our out- put was cut down by three hours a day; we had to discharge one set of girls; the remaining girls work four hours a day longer than they did formerly (and the regulations we were fringing were framed for the protection 0I female workers); and we are not now able to execute, all the orders we had on hand for an article previously imported from Germany. This kind of thing, as an instance, points to the necessity of traders having some assurance that, temporarily, of course, the ordinary re- strictions will be made more adjustable, and' that the officials concerned will work in such s way as to assist traders to make their rearrange- ments work as efficiently as possible. J. S. TILLING.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-street, Warrington, .19th May, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>RECRUITING AND EARLIER</head>
        <p>RECRUITING AND EARLIER</p>
        <p>t :LOSING.</p>
        <p>Sir,—No doubt a report of the meeting of retailers and distributors convened by the Mayor, and held on Tuesday night, will appear in your columns; but I should be grateful if YOU will allow me an opportunity or emphasising one of the difficulties of replacing male bY female labour in shops touched upon by two speakers at the meeting, viz., the unduly long hours.</p>
        <p>These hours are very trying, and the source of much unrest among ,the trained assistants, and must prove more so to the untrained hafl whom it is proposed to introduce. In manY large towns retailers have combined and agreed on earlier closing during the war, as they find it impossible to obtain assistants owing to the long, hours, and from many quarters we hear 01 assistants who have joined His Majesty's Fore&amp; declare they will never return to the trying oen- ditions and late hours of shop life. Long before the war tradespeople found an increasing diffi- culty in getting suitable assistants for this very reason. Young people naturally prefer going te the works and offices because of the shorter hours there. The great purchasing public are getting used' , to the unusual conditions of these abnornin' times, and I feel sure we may rely on theul doing what they can by shopping earlier t° relieve a worthy class of people whose present hours leave no time for recreation. But more than this is necessary. Cannot some influence be brought to bear on the Markets Committee to close the Markets earlier? The shops wilt gladly follow suit. I hope your columns will be open to more letters on this subject, an" that some definite action may be taken ill Consequence. RETAILER..</p>
        <p>RURAL ROUTlNE.—Continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>"The Retort Courteous." The item thus headed in last week's issue somehow brought to my mind an amusing in- cident of the days of auld lang sync. Once "hen I was in attendance as reporter at the ev°lll'4 Court, the • clerk to the then School -6 roard of that district was called as a witness 11 a ease of some importance. Counsel on the other Side was instructed by a local solicitor Whose father had been a tallow chandler, I Inention that as showing how, very tactless ,was his prompting of the barrister whom he nad retained when he suggested that in 'cross- tt"amination lie should endeavour to check the liblesome - witness's assurance by a re'. liiinder as to his humble parentage, " Let i 111.: scel" said coungel, " you .seem to consider rilcself a person of some consequence in this but isn't all this rather of mushroom owth ?" " Pin afraid I don't quite ..follow rii," responded the witness, with well- eeigned guilelessness. " Well, I'll be more filelic.it," proceeded the lawyer, looking e0n- t3,,"1-437 for the effect of his bomb; " it's net e„- years ago, you know, since you were ii-i",aged •in an entirely different occupation ;I'l.l. your father? Isi.ow, then, sir,' will you l'hease tell the Court what your father did in not very remote days ?" " Certainly," 48„tho prompt rejoinder. 11 When the father "le solicitor who instructs you, was making nry father was making candlesticks to them in !" The result may well be left to reader's imagination. • ,</p>
        <p>What Shall We Drink?" ti :rile timely recipe appearing last week under ,licadi,Pg! recalled for\ me a little incident j'eii was more laughable because of the Isiii.rer air and evident earnestness of -the than on account Of what be said. thol`e,iin a railway journey about the time of ent-- BHort-li'ved super-tax on intoxicants, I a-:er,cd into conversation on that subject with a:erious-looking stranger. With an emphasis farently intended to impress me with the importance •of the crisis so far as he al;"8 Personally concerned; my new acquaint- Cc said, " I don't know how it may be likely 1 ha affect you, of course,' but I assure you I 4re, scrutinised these proposals narrowly and C„l9usly „ to see, if the Chancellor had any ileasfigns on my favourite drink, and I .was rallY much relieved to find that he had 11,•+—.1coked it or failed to realise his opportu- in that direction." " Then you are ala`.'aced fortunate., and I congratulate you," " By-he-bye, you didn't mention was that had escaped the eagle eye of fa-,*. 14103'd George. What is the name of your hifnurite tipple ?" " Barley water," he -re- Ile.(1, without moving a muscle. He wasn't d°4ing either. The Problem of Pain. , found the short report of the special ser- -sain at -Cairo-street :Unitarian Church highly slitggestive. Many years ago I was forcibly ruck by the significance of this confession: ! 8,.," I used to think the inscriptions on grave-! b'unes intensely commonplace. Since I buried iflY child and' put a gravestone over her there oi`f uot an inscription which has not been full !limning to me." ;I,l'lle indeed it is that limitation when one —rives against the barriers, ;physical or !Piritual, is pain; yet if we never knew- suffer- 1-rilg how much sympathy should we learn? .I,et me extract a couple of apt sentences from , '2'B. Havelock-Ellis' s "Three Modern' .eet., 17 'How many people do you know who have ueeti truly helped 'in' the big, broad, human li(nse 'except by those who have suffered ?" It is only the resentment against suffering, the unwillingness to accept it Es a lesson in %tile unseen service, that causes its real 41' ." A oh tnidfl " The Vineyard," John Oliver serves: c,lderlyl °J. Young people is not common in rare—a",sB,;3lllB. Peal sympathy with youth (.)11 ohe,B tellehing quality, which depends exPerienee agination, but even more on one's IlaPpine ' and less on one's experience of difficultiBB than on one's experience of who are kesinaned, disappointment, lor the pe)opie Who 1 dt to the young arc usually those A 6 . undei sorrow:" ",a,filY, hard as it is to learn the lesson it : uanY, pain has its uses, and, like Peace, VietOrieS, too. Su rely Unique. !mei °ming -across the head of an old-estab- tol"ed firm of tailors in a small Lancashire 0 1%.-ni that has sent several hundreds of Men active service, I asked how business was await. 111111. To my surprise, my old acquaint-, ;lee assured me, beaming contentedly at the _etrospw, u that it had thus far this season 4retually exceeded what he did during the cer- lesPonding period last year. He was con- ,e4sedly unable to account for the phenomenon ill? face of the circumstance that so many of is old customers are at the, Front, and Could lint in the nature of things profess to be at all trguine as to the not distant ;future; still, singularly gratifying fact -remained that tiir the present and in immediate prospect the :118 upon the resources of his establishment el'e unmistakably above the !average.. I oe met few such happy men of late. One r al, the feW is a jeweller, and I •nay as well tell 'cut him while on the subject. !</p>
        <p>Ihr,„ 1-"oent and Understandable Taste. jeWeller friend made a little revelation hs-u loss interesting in its way. He showed that there is just now quite a boom in war 411, blenis of an ornamental character. Rela- ea.:eB °f our gallant fighting men, having taken ith`e to possess themselves of souvenirs in the thaPe of buttons or uniforni facings, have hpe„sn set or' otherwise treated so as to make •decoration for the dress. .For 1C,,• MPle, a soldier's baton—which, after all, he'nst popular and suitable as an article of t4lnment---is first gilded according to the 114„t of the owner and then mounted for latlY use as a pin of a brooch. k, A . "ord of Remonstrance. lav-esii from reading a certain paragraph in to " week's, Lymm news, I make opportunity 4hrall seriotip attention to a highly objection- ' 4i4 Practice' on the part of individuals here • there, and to earnestly appeal .to readers column to discourage it always and fa• 'f3'where. I refer to the systematic mann- ofe,;l,;re and circulation of mischievous, stories ft. "eged harm to brave men rho have gone this district to do valiant' service On .the , of honour in the sacred cause of liberty tilg,bteousness. There is no need to par- ali-4rise• It is a too prevalent habit, and II who are likely to read this must repeatedly tt°.been first alarmed and then indignant at sol-„ 1418 e rumours deliberately set 'afloat by laanenviable person or persons during the - Lew Weeks. 'The origin of so many of oier naturally startling but groundless tales oereParable • personal loss is attributable h 003,„ t 0 • a strangely perverted sense of th —'lll‘. and one cannot but think that if indulging in this diabolical form of sport thet.e "Pable,of realising how peculiarly cruel ('• effects of their senseless joking are in the .of auxions relatives of those whom with- foundation they have reported killed or elilmind,"d (as if, forsooth, we had not trouble Aineatig" of the kind), they would in penitence shame turn their ingenuity to more • etotable and profitable account. Of. course, they prefer to be reckoned among the toT• lies of their native land, there is no more said sop clone—unless, indeed, it were 8111 e to convict one or more of the offence, and give them their deserts.</p>
        <p>The Frugal Scot. That tit-bit of "Uncle N , about the</p>
        <p>Continued in Next Column,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 22, 1915.</p>
        <p>THE SHOP ASSISTANTS' CHANCE.</p>
        <p>E implorers Pledge Themselves to Help Recruiting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>EVERY AVAILABLE lIMAII TO BE RELEASED.</head>
        <p>EVERY AVAILABLE lIMAII TO BE RELEASED.</p>
        <p>That this meeting, representing the wholesale and retail distribution trades of Warrington, pledges itself to do everything in its power to help recruiting, and will, so far as possible, release every available man to serve with the colours and reinstate him on his return.</p>
        <p>The resolution quoted above was unanimously passed at a conference of representatives of the wholesale and retail distributing trades, held in the Council Chamber of the ToAkn Hall on Tues- day evening.. The meeting, at the request of Lord Kitchener and the Home Office, was called by the .11fayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), • and it was addressed by Mr. F. W. Goldstone, M.P., a Labour member, and One of the Parliamentary Recruiting Committee, who 'made a compelling appeal, in the course of which he emphasisell the fact that, in the .matter of getting recruits, "the kind of cempiasied we want is the benevo- lent compulsion of a. generous encouragement." In addition to the Mayor and the principal speaker there were present: Aldermen John Evans, A. Bennett and D. Tinnion, Colonel Meggridge, Lieutenant-Colonel Mocatta, Major Pole, Lieutenant Williamson, Mr. A. T. Halla- way •(deputy town clerk), Mr. T. H. Spinks (assistant town clerk), the Chief Constable (Mr. M. Nicholls), Mr. J. M. Murray (director of education), and the following representatives of local firms: Warrington Co-operative Society, Mr: J. Jarman; J. Banks_ (outfitter), Mr.' J. Banks; W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., Messrs. W. and A. Hodgkinson ; _Mr. T. Domville, pawnbroker; Warrington Rural District Council, Mr. B. S. Newcombe; Carter and Son, Ltd., Mr. Stanley Carter; Henry Milling and Co., -Ltd., Mr. Henry Milling; J. Critchley (tailor), Mr. James Critch- ley ; Mr. E. A.-Sanders, Liberal agent; J. Povey and Son, •Ltd., - Mr. James Povey ; Edwin Richardson and Son, Ltd., Mr. John E. Richard- son. and Mr. John Jackson; Tilling, Gray and Co., Ltd., Mr. J. S. Tilling; Mr. Jas. Bate, fruit merchant;. Mr. George C. Miller, fruit mer- chant; S. N. Green and Co., Ltd., Mr. Sam N. Green; Greenall, Whitley and Co., Mr. Joseph H. Clarke; -Mr. Thomas Partridge, imperial Hotel; Mr. Harry Rogers, Friars' Gate; J. B. Isaac and Co., Mr. B. Bennett and .Mr. A. Pendlebury ; G. Davey-Smith, King-street, Mr. G. Davey Smith; the Home and Colonial Stores, Ltd., Mr. W. Bew ; Public Benefit Boot. Co., Mr. A. Price; Stead and Simpson, Ltd., Mr. T. H. Tretwell ; A. Mynekyme, Mr. F. J. Myne-. kyme ; J. Nelson and Son, Mr. P. S. Ward; and Shop Assistants' -Union (Warrington Branch), Mr. E. Thomas.</p>
        <p>COUNTRY CALLING LOUDLY FOR MEN. By way of prelnde to the meeting, the Mayor said that, whilst he did not think that any particular coullflaint could be laid at the door of. any trade, the War Office and the Country were calling loudly for More men, and he was afraid that the call would not be stopped until some additional power was given ,to the country, or until the war was settled. The circular calling the meeting had been addressed to the shopkeepers and business gentlemen who were in the distributing trade, and he felt sure that whatever effort they could put forth in reference t,o this great war they would do so cheerfully, as WarringtonianS always did. The appeal would not fall on deaf ears, and, at any cost to themselves, they would do what they could to help on the recruiting Iflove7 ment, a matter in which the distributing trades of Warrington would not be behind .the .dis- tributing trades in any other place. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>300,000 MORE MEN WANTED—AT ONCE! Mr. Goldstone prefaced a lengthy address by saying that those present were already con- vinced of the greatness of the issue, and in. order to carry the war through, as soon as pos- sible, to a completely successful termination, the Government, acting through the various departments, were attempting to raise more men. Only that •day Lord Kitchener, in the House of Lords, had made an appeal for 300,000 more men, in addition to those who had already enlisted. It was hoped td get them by taking stock of the existing trades and indus- tries and, thus finding out the Sources of -un- tapped material.. .If that attempt failed, it seemed to him that no matter what their views might be, from an academic standpoint, respecting voluntaryism or conscription, the main issue would be borne in upon them, and that was that they had to win this war at all costs, and although, perhaps, .the majority of those present: hoped that the necessary men would be forthcoming voluntary, he thought all of them were prepared to face the sinking of individual views as to the method of raising men if it were a question of the issue hanging in the ,balance. At Present the view of the Government seemed to' be that the various trades and industries should be approached through local channels to. find .out what men there were who were still available and who, by taking the 'proper steps, might become members of his Majesty's forces. •</p>
        <p>face a little. less celevity than the shop assist- ants had been accustomed to give; it was the sacrifice that the customer was called upon to make! The feeling of the women in this coun- try was so keen about the war that those who Offered their services were fully determined so to qualify themselves that, to the best of their ability, the labour they could give would be no whit behind that of the men whom they re-- leased. The alternative, and- to some extent the corollary, was the employment of men of non-military age; and he thought they had got to the stage where, when a man of military ago went to the Front, they were justified in imlvertising for another of non-military age. So they might centre their thoughts around the possibility of releasing men and the method whereby the places of the men .could be filled. The kind of compulsion flint was wanted in the present case was the benevolent compulsion of a generous encouragement; not, at present, the direct compulsion of telling a man that be must gb, -and not even going to the extent of enter- ing his name on the list which employers would be asked to fill in—a list on which they would be required to state the number of men of military age Whom they still employed and the number of those whom they estimated could be released.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Trade That Has Done Its Share.</head>
        <p>Trade That Has Done Its Share.</p>
        <p>He hoped those present would not assume that because they were called together that evening that there was any intention of suggesting that the distributing and retail trades had not done their duty. So far froth that being the case, shop assistants, commercial travellers, clerks and warehousemen had, probably, done so, well that no other trade had surpassed them. No fewer than 260,000 shop assistants had enlisted —“cheers)—but it was estimated that 220,000 of military age still remained, eligible or outside, or, possibly, were so tied up that they could not be spared. It was estimated that some- thing like 430,000 men from the particular trade- to which he referred—shop assistants, warehousemen, clerks and the rest—had enlisted, leaving behind an estimated number of 360,000. ~The problem resolved itself into this: Could they by taking thought and counsl together absolve a considerable portion of those Men from their ordinary duties, so .that they might have the opportunity of serving with the Forces? The _question which faced them that</p>
        <p>night was coula they do anything to re-arrange their various businesses so as to free a con- siderable number of men? What could be done? So. far as part of the retail trade was concerned they could recruit a coniderable number of women to take the places of the men who were at present employed by them. To do that the. Board of Trade, through the Labour Exchanges, were 'compiling .tt register of women who were prepared to take industrial duty so that the men Might be free for the pur- poses of the War.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>Female Labour Still To Be Utilised.</head>
        <p>Female Labour Still To Be Utilised.</p>
        <p>In this connection he believed that, if active efforts were made now, the short list already_ in existence at the Labour Exchange would be very considerably lengthened; for there was A considerable body of female labour still to be utilised. - In this time of the country's need, the speaker contitined, Customers must be pre- pared, and, he believed, would be prepared, to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.-</p>
        <p>Continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <p>thrifty Scotsman reminded me • of several others like unto it, but I fear me lest publica- tion should bring a fierce demand for my head on a charger. I read, that at a certain Brotherhood gathering the other week; • the chairman remarked on the international char- acter of the platform. A Welshman had sung a 'solo, a Frenchman (Pasteur. Henri D'Aubigne), was giving the address an Irish- man sat on his right hand, and ;here were three Cockneysl near by. " Can't you find a Scotchman?" asked a voice from the audi- ence. $‘ He left just before the collection," was the chairman's jocular „reply. The audi- ence laughed heartily, but since the meeting the ehairman has had many letters vehemently protesting against the libel. So I reluctantly leave my Poor little anecdotes where they are —at any rate, for the present. Or is it. that my -space is exhausted? Well, that is cer- tainly a reinarliable coincidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>Patriotic Shop Assistants.</head>
        <p>Patriotic Shop Assistants.</p>
        <p>They would be asked whether they would re- quire substitutes—women, or men of non-mili- tary age—and also whether they were willing to guarantee reinstatement, if it was desired, to enlisted men? It had come to the knowledge of the Parliamentary Recruiting Committee, from the, householders' returns, that no less than 22,000 shop assistants had expressed their wil- lingness to enlist if a guarantee could be given them of reinstatement after the -sar was over. Ile suggested to those present that possibly they could help to. the extent of giving that guarantee. and if they did so they would relieve the difficulty of the position for a number of men who were not slackers, but who were, rather, looking at ,the responsibilities they had at home, and who were just wondering—and lie could not blame Them—what was to happen to those totally dependent upon .them if they came back to find the place they used to fill was filled by someone else.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>Sacrifice Demanded from Everyone.</head>
        <p>Sacrifice Demanded from Everyone.</p>
        <p>He thought every one of them was convinced of the rightness of the. British cause; every one was deeper in earnest to sacrifice everything that they could, so that the country might go through and emerge successfully from the war, not only from the point pf view of national interests, but also from the standpoint of a higher interest—the interest of the preservation for the world of civilisation as against the brute force which the enemy were exhibiting in the most callous forms.. If that were their inten- tion, it seemed to him that each one of them had to face some amount of sacrifice in excess of that already faced, and, the sacrifice which might be called for from those in the particular trade to which they belonged might very well be that of so reorganising their forces- as ,to free that. fraction of the 360,1)00 men that was required, and that was represented in War- rington. (Applause.) Mr. J. E. Richardson said it had been sug- gested that female--guards might take the place of male guards on the tramways, but the trade unions had said " No," and had refused to work under , such conditions. Might not the same thing obtain with the shopkeepers if they adopted a similar course? Mr. E. Thomas of .the Shop Assistants' Union, said that &amp;his 'Union ,would have no objection whatever on the question of female labour, provided the woman was paid three- quarters of the wage received by the man, whose, place she was taking, and that. the men returned to their places after the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>AN ECONOMIC DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>AN ECONOMIC DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. .Jarman said that the members of. the local branch •of the Co-op Employes' Union, expressed no objection whatever to replacing men by women, and no stipulation had been Made" with regard ..to wages. • Perhaps, the reason for that was that the Co-oP Society already had .a scale in operation for .female labour, but though he was positive that there was no rate for women higher that the Co- operative Society's present rate, it would not work Out at three-fourths of a man's wages, be- cause a man of 21 in the employment of the Co- operative Society received 28s. a week, whilst the rate for a girl of 20 was 17s. per week.. It was a- case in which there should be give and take, and he believed that, for 'a time at any rate, it would be necessary to replace one man with two women. In that event it would mean a loss instead of gain to the employer, so far as wages were concerned, because they knew that much of the work could not be done as readily by women as by men. If they paid two women a guinea each per week to replace a man who had been earning 28s: per week, business could not go .u.n. Tie suggested that the matter should, within reasonable limits, be left for the shopkeepers to arrange, but a minimum should perhaps be fixed.</p>
        <p>- Mt. 11. Milling said that if the shopping hours were shortened it would be easier for the trades- people to release more men for military service.</p>
        <p>THE DILATORY SHOPPER.</p>
        <p>Air. Jarman: What you want is a town's meet- ing that will get hold of the people who will come late shopping and tell them just exactly what you think of them. Our hours at the Co- op Stoges are less than anybody's in town, hut there are people who will conic in just as the door is closing.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Critchley said that there were posi- tions in the shops and warehouses which ,no woman could take up, and in that case the only alternative would be the employment of Men who were over military age. Mr— Tilling said that with -regard to the employment of women, the traders would want Some guarantee that they would not be " hum- bugged about." by factory inspectors. lie knew of cases in which girls had taken the place of Youths and the working liJurs had had to be reduced three hours per. day. 4 A questioner. asked Mr. Goldstone what objection he had to women being paid full wages if they did the work of a man? -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>DIFFICULTY THAT OCCURS.</head>
        <p>A DIFFICULTY THAT OCCURS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Goldstone replied that be, personally, had no objection. On the other hand he would like to see them so paid, but he was looking at it from the point of view of the men to whom he was appealing. "If," he added, "- they have to pay women at the same rate as men, and at the seine time grant the pen 'something to eke out the, separation allowance, it may make all the difference between being able to carry on or having to close up. I want to see them carry on. No one can .feel more acutely than I do the desirability of safeguarding the trade union position, but I have not yet heard of an employer who is seek;ng permanently to alter the • standard Which has beep attained. For the moment we have to approach the prob- lem from the standpoint of national security, while obtaining Incidentally the best conditions for the men who go." As to the suggestion re the earlier closing of the markets, Mr. Gold- stone added that he believed that, if the diffi- culty were at all widespread, representations to the Government would be sympathetically con- sidered.</p>
        <p>DIFFICULTY OF SAYING " YOU MUST GO OR GET OUT."</p>
        <p>Mr. Jarman then proposed the resolution already, quoted and, in doing so, said that a number of the employes of the Co-operative Society had enlisted, but he ciould say, without hesitation, that a number of others might do so if they chose. The meeting could well under- stand the position of a large employer who Was not prepared to say to his men "You must go or get out." The fact that the firms would now have ,to supply: figures as to eligible men Might help the Government in taking sonic 'steps in the matter which they would otherwise not see their way to take. The Co-operative Society undertook that any eMploye Who joined the Forces should, on his return, receive at least the same rate Of wages he bad previously</p>
        <p>been paid, although they could not guarantee that he should have._exactly the same Position. They were prepared to do all they possibly could with women labour if they could- not</p>
        <p>ibtain men over military age. There might .be i,ome difficulty in adjusting the question of A-ages, but he did not think it would be in- aiperable. The Society also made up the</p>
        <p>difference between tile Government allowance and the wages of employes who had dependents.</p>
        <p>OLD MEN IN KHAKI-SLACKERS STILL WEARING "CIVVY'S" CLOTHES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richardson seconded, and, in doing so, said that if he had any difficulty in the matter at all, it was the feeling he had at the back of his mind that the object of the meeting was that the screw should be put on by the employer, while the nation still adhered to the voluntary system. Though he was disappointed with the response made by, the young men at his own establishment, lie did not feel that lie wanted. to call them up and appeal to them a second time. The position was such that it ought to appeal to them without anything being done by the employer. If there was one thing that made him sad, it was to see men approaching 45 or 50 years of fine marching through the streets in khaki, when lie knew of the number Of "slackers" about the town. He had a, feel- ing that the Government were indirectly ask- ing employers to do something which they them- selves had failed to do—to tell the young men that they must go. II is firm, in filling vacan- cies, would ask' that the men engaged should be over the military age. Mr. Richardson added that if the employers had to employ two women to take the place of every man and pay them on the hard and fast lines mentioned by the Shop Assistants' Union, it would be driving a hail. into the coffin of the proposals made that night, because it would make the carrying on of busi- ness an absolute, impossibility. The proposition- was then put and carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>-WHERE THE BEST FIRMS GO FOR HANDS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, in reply to a remark which had ,een made by Mr. Hiehardson—to the effect that</p>
        <p>when he asked .a lady who was desirous of working whether she had registered her name at the Labour Exchange; he got the reply " No! None of the good firms go there !"—said : "You can take it from Me that that lady evi- dently did not know anything about it. . The best firms do go there and get satisfied every day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>UNUSUAL BUSINESS-NOT "BUSINESS AS USUAL."</head>
        <p>UNUSUAL BUSINESS-NOT "BUSINESS AS USUAL."</p>
        <p>Colonel Moggridge proposed a, vote .of thanks to the Mayor and to Mr. Goldstone, and in doing so said that he had been busy for nine: months trying—and succeeding very well—to get men to enlist. The one great bar to securing a greater response seemed to him to be the cry that was started at the beginning of the war, and which was still . heard, "Business as usual." He • did not see how anybody could expect business to be as usual in these abso- lutely abnormal times. That cry had pre- vented people understanding. as they should the very critical times through which we were passing. People were -trying to carry on as usual, and it could not be done. He was Cer- tain those present Would help in every way to substitute female, for male labour. It was only temporary measure; it was a thing which he did not suppose would ever be asked again. A very large number of shop assistants were of the right age and physically' fit, and the mili- tary 'authorities Would like them to come in. Alderman Tinnion, in seconding, referred to the great interest the Mayor had taken in re- cruiting work, and added that he felt sure that the object the .Government had in view, would be attained so far as Warrington was concerned. The proposition was carried with applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>GOVERNMENT'S INTENTION.</head>
        <p>THE GOVERNMENT'S INTENTION.</p>
        <p>In reply Mr. Goldstone said that so far as he was coOerned there was no suggestion that employers should apply the screw. He gathered . .</p>
        <p>that the intention- Of. the Government- was to exhaust all possible means of preserving the voluntary system. If the efforts in the various trades and industries failed to bring the men who were absolutely necessary, it seemed to him that no Government could afford to go on unless they were prepared to take the utmost means to get the men required to see the war through to the end they all desired. (Applause.) , The Mayor also expressed his thanks, saying that he felt that at a time like that they could not do enough, and adding that if ever he could do anything to help recruiting, and assist the Government in the hour of trial he felt that he ought to do it, and that he deserved no thanks for doing it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>WILL U.S.A. REMAIN ON THE FENCE?</head>
        <p>WILL U.S.A. REMAIN ON THE FENCE?</p>
        <p>Warringlonian's Views On America's</p>
        <p>\Vat. Allitude.</p>
        <p>' Mr. William Broadhurst, of 77, Priest- ley-street, Warrington, • has received an interesting letter from his son-in-law, Mr. R. Purslow, who, with his wife, is at present residing at Rokbury, Alass.,.and who describes the effect of the war on American trade, and also throws some sidelights on (minion in the U.S.A. " Work here," writes Mr. Purslow, is very bad, there being scarcely anything doing I should like you to see some of the cartoons of the Kaiser published in the American Press. . . One 9f our judges remarked that he would ,like to see a dozen bombs fall on the head of the Kaiser and his advisers._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>Germans' Petition to President.</head>
        <p>Germans' Petition to President.</p>
        <p>The Germans here went nearly mad over the judge's remark and petitioned the President to remove the offending justice from the Bench. Fancy removing a judge just to please a handful of Huns. lam glad to say that Woodrow Wilson told the Germans that he could not listen to them. . The less England tampers with American trade the bet- ter it will be for them over here. The real Americans are all right, but the Hibernians, the Germans and the Austrians have a lot of influence, and all over this great country they are working and planning to.bff-set England's plans."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>How the Huns' Submarines Get Their</head>
        <p>How the Huns' Submarines Get Their</p>
        <p>Information.</p>
        <p>The writer goes on to refer to the recent visit of a German cruiser to Newport News, and opines that the object of the visit was to get in touch with German agents in the States, with a view to arranging for the export of coal and provisions, and also to ascertain the time and date of sailing of any. English or French ships, and to discover the precise courses they were to take, in order that they might communicate the information to the three German commerce 'raiders who were scouring the Atlantic. "The same sort of thing I am sure," the writer adds, "is taking place round the British Isles, the work of ascertain- ing the information &amp;c. necessary being obtained by Germans from America, who speak good English, and pass as either Englishmen or Irishmen, and who pose. as fishermen, but are in reality runners for the German sub- marines, letting them .know what boats are leaving Liverpool, Queenstown, &amp;c. .. . lam really afraid you people over there are about a hundred years behind America and Germany in some respects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>America Peculiarly Situated.</head>
        <p>America Peculiarly Situated.</p>
        <p>"Bothithese countries can run you off the face of the earth in the matter of electrical equip- meats, and they are always trying to improve on them. . . You think America will soon be involved in the war. I do not think so. America is peculiarly fixed and there are many 'big reasons why she should not implicate herself but should remain strictly neutral, . • . Work 16 very poor here. There are thousands of men loafing around starving. . . . (hood luck to Our gallant boys at the Front, and bad luck to the crack-brained Kaiser, who I hope soon to see at St. Helena."</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CRICKETER'S MARRIAGE.</p>
        <p>In a letter to Mr. Walter BAarley, the in- teresting news is conveyed by Mr. 11. G. Gannett that he was married. The event took place quietly inst atter the bridegroom had lett hospital. The wound front whielt he suffers has not yet healed, however. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>BOOKS AND MAGAZINES.</head>
        <p>BOOKS AND MAGAZINES.</p>
        <p>Life and Writings of Alfred-Lord Tennyson. By Arthur Turnbo. Creat writers' series. Critical studies of the world's - greatest authors. London: The Walter Scott Publishing Co., Ltd. is. net.</p>
        <p>The author of this little book sets out for the. reader an admirable epitome of the _</p>
        <p>several 'lives of the great Laureate of the Vic- torian era, written by Hallam, the present Lord Tennyson, and other distinguished authors—the leading facts in the career of the poet gathered from these authentic sources, being set forth concisely and in an orderly arrangement, which for the student furnishes a comprehensive history of the various stages of development by which the apostle of poetic culture attained the height of his fame.</p>
        <p>Mr. Turnbull also gives many excerpts from well-known writers who have criticised the poet's style and methods, and binds the whole :together with comments of his own on many of the principal poems. - There is a copious index, and a chronology of the poems. The get-up of the book does great credit to the publishers.</p>
        <p>Man and Woman. By Havelock Ellis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>London: The Walter Scott Publishing Co.; ___</head>
        <p>London: The Walter Scott Publishing Co.; ___</p>
        <p>Ltd. 6s. net</p>
        <p>This is a new edition of Mr. Havelock Ellis's well-known work. The book is revised and greatly enlarged. In it the author gives us the results of many years' research into the differences between the sexes, disclosing facts of immense social import. Reports of inveS- tigations made in various countries by eminent scientists throw light on many ques- tions much to the fore lately. Peculiarly in- teresting are the results of investigations made. with a view to discovering the mental and physical differences between boys and girls of various ages.</p>
        <p>Mr. Havelock Ellis quickly disposes of dis- cussion on the " alleged inferiority of women " by pointing out the futility of such !discussion, and he rightly declines to bar the way of future progress by pre-conceived notions of the fitness of women for particular spheres of activity. " We are not at liberty," he ;says, " to introduce any artificial sexual barrier into social concerns. The respective fitness of men and of women for any kind of work or any kind of privilege\ can only be ascertained by actual open experiment; and as the 'conditions for such experiment are never twice the same, it can never be posi- tively affirmed that anything has been settled. once and for all. When such experiment is successful, so much the•better for the race; when it is unsuccessful, the minority who have broken natural law alone suffer. An exaggerated anxiety lest natural law be over- thrown is misplaced. The world is not so in- securely poised. We may preserve an atti- tude of entire equanimity in the face of social readjustment. Such readjustment is either the outcome of wholesome natural instinct, in which case our social structure will be strengthened and broadened, or it is not ;. and if not, it is unlikely to become organically in- grained in the species." _</p>
        <p>The book mas- be 'cordially commended to all who are seriously interested in the social problems of our times. It is a mine Of in- formation bearing on these great questions, presented in that calm and judicial manner it is the special pride of the scientist to Pre- serve amid the conflicts of jarxing factions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>GREECE AND THE ENTENTE POWERS.</head>
        <p>GREECE AND THE ENTENTE POWERS.</p>
        <p>Of pro-German feeling in Greece there is practically none. The case of the 'Allies has been well presented, and from the 'senti- mental point of view the nation is without doubt, in favour of England and France. Turkey is the hereditary enemy, and the. thrust of Austria towards Salonika has long been a menace. All that caq be said on the other side is that to Germany itself there is no. actual ill-feeling, and that some personal gratitude is felt to the Kaiser for the part he played in crying " Han&amp; off" to Austria at the end of the second Balkan War. It is when we come to the question of self-interest that ,things are not so simple. Greece has already passed through two trying struggles within less than three years. One of its principal industries is its growing mercantile marine, with a tonnage of about 400,000. With Austria put out of action, and English ttnd French steamers largely used for war purposes, the carrying trade of the Mediter- ranean is passing into the hands of Italy and Greece. It is a temptation for the Greek commercial classes to ,welcome this chance of recouping themselves for the sacrifices 9f the Balkan Wars.—Professor Ronald. Burrows in the " Contemporary Review " for May.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>WELSH WOMEN ROUSED.</head>
        <p>WELSH WOMEN ROUSED.</p>
        <p>- An interesting interview with. Mrs. Lloyd George appears in " Great Thoughts" for May (Smith's Publishing Co., 6d.)—the first number of a new volume. Speaking of Welsh women and the war the wife of the Chancel- lor says: "The treatment of Belgium by the Germans has roused the Welsh women indeed. Then, too, they are not now afraid of being left a burden to others. or the parish, as they were at The start of the war. The Govern- men't's generous arrangements have freed them from that fear. In some villages I know, where men at first hung back, after the women saw what good money came regu-: larly to the families of the few who had gone, when they grasped that the wives and chil- dren left behind were actually better off, financially; than. they themselves whose men- •folk were still working at home these women soon began—it makes me laugh to think of it, but we Welsh people do like such a good joke !—these women soon began to urge their</p>
        <p>own husbands and sons to join the Forces too! And to-day there is hardly a man left in those villages who could possibly be accepted a- soldier, sailor, or combatant of some kind!" Discussingi the drink problem Mrs. Lloyd George says: " I am not sure that restric- tion of hours of sale will do much to solve the problem. lam told by good authorities such a course only means that those who want drink will buy it in bottles, so as to have it when the ' pubs ' are closed. lam more in- clined to suPport lessening• by far the num- ber of licensed houses. A man or woman will not generally walk miles just to get a drink."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>MAY "WOMAN AT HOME."</head>
        <p>THE MAY "WOMAN AT HOME."</p>
        <p>The Early Summer Fashions Number forms the May issue of " The Woman at Home," and special emphasis is given to fashions for matrons. The interview with Miss Laurette Taylor, who is quite inseparable from " Peg o' my Heart,': will be read with a great deal of pleasure. "A Woman's Career," by the late Myrtle Reed, will be much appreciated by readers of the " Woman at Home " ; J. E. Buckrose, the author of "Down our Street," contributes a fascinating story under the title "' The Old Bonnet Shop: A Millinery Secret." In lighter vein E. L. Sabin delights us with a long story entitled " A Cold in the Head." " 'War Professions for Women," by. Fay Lankester, tells what excellent openings Public Health Work offers to well educated and trained women. Long instalments of the serial stories; " The Great German Plot," by Alan Graham, and "Should She Have Told Him?" and the many articles of home inter- et eGmplete a very interesting and useful number. •</p>
        <p>Essays and Verse. Wo have received " Essays' and Verse, by Abraham Cowley," a new volume of the well- known shilling library of Walter Scott Publidting C 0.,, Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <p>THE PILL-STEEL. BICYCLE</p>
        <p>by the use, of specially constructed tracking jigs, thus ensuring true running. Raleighs are guar- anteed for ever and fitted with Dunlop tyres and Sturnry-Archer 3-speed gear. Prices from</p>
