      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>FUTURE FOR CYCLING.</head>
        <p>FUTURE FOR CYCLING.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence • should. be addressed to The Rub," c'o the E.litnr. Replies cannot be sent by post, and .all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>WHY NOT ADVERTISE CYCLING?</head>
        <p>WHY NOT ADVERTISE CYCLING?</p>
        <p>• The glory of the open road is realised by every wheelman and wheelwoman.. But. the advantages of club cycling are not so aliparent. There is a great opportunity either to be taken or left, by cycling clubs throughout the country, after the war is Over. The thousands of men who have for the first time learnt to appreciate the real pleasure of open-air life, and who, will return to this country then, should be encouraged to join some club, so that they will have an opportunity of keeping up to a certain extent, the good they have received from living in the 'open. The pastime of cycling does not advertise itself. Why not? is a common question asked. I believe it would be productive of much good, if, when the war is finished, all clubs would advertise the fact that they exist, 'so as to -draw into the net those whom they want to benefit..._ Those who will return to their former occupations will suffer if they do not continue to find out of door exercise and recreation. The: only way to get hold of the greater 'number of these fellows is to advertise for them. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>BY-WAYS.</head>
        <p>THE BY-WAYS.</p>
        <p>For many years' past, but particularly during the last three or four seasons, I have been addicted to traversing the by-ways rather than the highways. This coming season will, I know, but strengthen me in my resolve to leave the main roads severely alone, as- far as possible, and take to the capillary roads with which this country abounds. I have two,. objections to find with the principal thorough- fares. In the first place, they are overrun by motor-cyclists and motor-cars; in the second the surfaces are far from good. I have suc- ceeded in the last two years in getting the secre- tary of the club to which I belong to plan his fixture list, so as to include the less frequented tracks, and I notice. from the published lists of various clubs in London, that they are now following our lead. The 1)Y-roads very often run parallel to the main roads, but they take in villages and hamlets which are teeming with inthest and delightfully old-worldish. Nearly always they are missed by the average rider. To do a hundred miles a day is nothing; to enjoy every moment spent awheel is the main object for all cyclists. • Try the side tracks and judge for yourself. THE LAW AGAINST US.</p>
        <p>We are generally told that the laws are made for the benefit of the majority. This may be true in certain directions, but it is not true in all. A case was recently brought to the Courts by a cyclist who was badly bitten by a a dog. Every rider runs the risk of being in-- jured in the same way. It is not only the vicious beasts that bite, but playful and stupid dogs very often attempt to do the same. The law on the subject is to the effect that unless the injured person is able to prove that the owner was aware of the dog's propensity to bite he has no redress. The action for damages was eventually settled out of Court. The cyclist had had to endure pain and suffer monetary loss, and, if it had not been for, the generosity of the dog owner, he would 'undoubtedly have received no. compensation. The owner, however, was generous and he gave the cyclist £5. This is only one instance of the law being</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>TRUTH ABOUT RUSSIAN</head>
        <p>TRUTH ABOUT RUSSIAN</p>
        <p>PROHIBITION.</p>
        <p>The exact nature and extent of the liquor prohibition in Russia is not, it seems, quite understood in this country. Many conflicting statements have appeared recently in the Press, some to the effect that the prohibition in Russia concerns only vodka, the. national Rus- sian spirit, and does not ban Wines and beers, while other statements have implied that the new law extends only to the rural districts, the towns and cities being free from any re- strictions.</p>
        <p>In view of these misconceptions it is .inter- esting to note a leading article in the "- Outro RoSsii," of Moscow, which deals with the sub- ject and states clearly the real position. The article, refers to a recent semi-official confer- ence in Petrograd, at which it was decided, to recommend the Government to allow the sale of beers and light wines. "This resolution," says the Moscow journal, "created a widespread anxiety which has now been allayed by the Tsar himself. On hearing of' the threatened re-opening of the beer houses the Temperance Society of Nizhni-Novgorod sent up, through its president, the Grand Duke Constantine Constantinovitch, an urgent peti- tion to the Tsar, in which he was urged not to abate the beneficial law of prohibition by allowing the sale of beers and wines. ~ "At the foot of the telegrani handed to him by the Grand Duke the Tsar inscribed with his own hand the following words: " There is no ground for anxiety. As long as the war lasts wines and beers will not be sold gener- ally." •</p>
        <p>The last word seenis.to leave •a loophole, a s it might imply that beers and wines may be allowed to be sold locally in the near future, but it is .quite clear that at present strict prohibition extending to all districts and all forms of alcohol is in force throughout Russia, as it has been for the past nine months.</p>
        <p>Mutual Reluctance. " Here is my seat, madam, but eandour pels nie-td say that 1 think you are as well able to stand as I am." "Politeness compels um to say Thank you,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <p>, •</p>
        <p>ca</p>
        <p>against the whole cycling community. When peace returns again we must agitate to have such regulations amended.</p>
        <p>SECOND-HAND MOTOR-CYCLES.</p>
        <p>There appears to be a very good demand this season for second-hand motor-cycles, and a large number of machines are changing hands. It is difficult for those who have never owned such a mount before to be able to judge the value and know how much should be paid. AN IMPROVEMENT WANTED.</p>
        <p>The majority of motor-cyclists agree that one improvement that is badly wanted on their machines is better rear-springing. I have had a number of letters recently. On this subject, the writer in each case mentioning this point. One correspondent . says, "My 1915 machine would be ideal if instead of being rigid the rear Was sprung." 'lt is a subject that has, entered the minds of many riders in the past, but evidently the awful condition of our principal roads has made their drawback more apparent this. season. Pot-holes, instead of being an occasional nuisance, are now a continual night- mare. One difficulty that. manufacturers 'are up against in springing the rear of the cycle to Make a satisfactory and economical job of it, is that such springing must require little or no running attention, and must be free from wear. To design a suitable spring frame embodying these .features is far from easy. If we are patient, however, I am eonvincedt a practical form of frame will be built some ddy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>PETROL-PRODUCING PROCESSES.</head>
        <p>PETROL-PRODUCING PROCESSES.</p>
        <p>, The problem of increasing the world's fuel supply is daily becoming a matter of more importance. The withqawal of horses, •on account of the war, has compelled the indus- trial community to take to motor traction at an earlier date than was expected. This has increased ,the demand. Again, the war calls for a tremendous quantity, a demand which must be supplied first of all. Chemists and mining engineers all over the .world are work- ing hard to discover new and cheap sources from which motor spirit may lib obtained. A number of new processes. have been discovered, but the difficulty in time past has been to pass the laboratory stage and to place production on a commercial basis: It is a big step between the two. An important question is that of re- ducing co-st. From what I can gather the main object is to treat the raw material by improved processes, so that it will yield -a greater quan- tity of motor spirit. One new" process is that by which it is possible to obtain petrol from synthetic crude oil. Paraffin, vaseline, rodwax and other natural hydrocarbons are reconverted into. crude oil and the, spirit obtained there- from. The Rittman cracking process is, claimed to give a larger percentage of spirit froms petroleum than , obtainable before. Cheaper petrol is a thing which appeals to all of us.</p>
        <p>CHEAPER CARS. ,</p>
        <p>People who buy cars want them to last as many seasons as possible. 31a nufacturers have therefore kept this point in view in designing their engines, chassis and body- work. They have not accepted the •Ameri- can idea that "just good enough" is good enough for them. 'They, have erred to a cer- tain extent on the other side. They have devoted their skill 'hi the pyoduction of a limited number of light cars f tip-top quality. Designers would do well to remember one thing, namely, that flue demand to-day is from the million who want a car at a reasonable price, which is trustworthy and built to last. These are directions :in which the cast of pro- duction could be reduced, thus extending the market. The all-British car to sell at ..1100 is still a thing of the future. We can not arrive at it all at once, but makers can take a step in the right direction. This is all the more important now, since the cost of labour and material has increased, and the tendency is for the price of the finished :produce to rise. This increase in price will still farther restrict the Market.</p>
        <p>TITE</p>
      </div>
