      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <p>4 , ,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 22, 1915.</p>
        <p>ttiv??????l't4:4ltt*:*??4:4.• ter:***et•:**???•:•*????++??+ •4:• *??+ +t4 +••:•+4-2•4"4 ••:••••:**:••:••:**:*+•:**:••:r ....................................4....... .. 4..................4.......4.......... 4. ... ... ......4. 4.......4.4....... 4.4.4....4. 4.4.4. +4-+ 4.4. 4.4. 4.4.4. 4. 4. 4. 4.4, 4.4. 4. 4. 4. .4.4. 4.4. .4.4. 4. 4:. 4.4. 4.4. 4.4. 4.4. 4.4. 4. 4.</p>
        <p>horticulture, market gardening, and agricul- ture. These committees will be in close touch with the Government Central Commit- tee, and the various Government Horticultural and Agricultural Colleges. Berkshire boasts already one of these committees, which owes much to the co-operation of University College, Reading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Tijurrli Arius.</head>
        <p>Tijurrli Arius.</p>
        <p>SINS OF THE YOUNG MAN.</p>
        <p>(Attire Instead Of Cricket ; Books Instead Of 'Bacca.</p>
        <p>The sins of the young man were cried from the pulpit of Cairo-street Church on Sunday evening. .He spends his money on tobacco -in- stead of books; he talks cricket instead of cultivating his mind; he goes to. picture palaces when he would be better employed at night school; and he grows up a dissatisfied cynic; so at any rate, says the Rev. Chas. Roper, :of West Kirby, who preached the Sunday school anniversary sermons. He contended that it should be the great aim of the teacher and the parent to alter this state of affairs by influenc- ing the children to take a delight in culture for its own sake.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>Education and Religion.</head>
        <p>Education and Religion.</p>
        <p>Taking as his text: " The fear of the Lord is the beginning - of knowledge: but fools despise wisdom and instruction." (Proverbs, i., 7). • The rev, gentleman, at the outset, pointed to /the importance of education in religion. True religion was true life. The man who claimed to be religious muSt show himself to be a good man, and the man whose intellectual pavers were developed according to the. opportunities that came his way was better equipped to lead a good life than him whose education had been neglected. Education helped to create a more enobling conception of God and human duty. The advance of civilisation had been propor- tionate to the advance in human knowledge. The reverend gentleman went on to deplore the smallness of the amount spent by our nation on education. We were, he alleged, being beaten commercially and industrially, by nations that valued education more highly than we did.. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>An Army of Grumblers.</head>
        <p>An Army of Grumblers.</p>
        <p>The majority of the young men of seventeen and . eighteen who were educated at our colleges and secondary schools thought far too much of cricket and football and far too little of intellectual pursuits. And when they at- tained to man's estate they formed a large army of grumblers. They stood in the way of truth and progress, and took no interest in any of the things that mattered. They denied that there was a good God ruling in the universe, just because He hadn't strewn their particular paths .with roses—because He hadn't suspended the time-old law that whatsoever a man sows he will reap. If the young man would spend at night school the time he spent now in picture palaces; if he would spend on books the money he now spent on tobacco and cigarettes, it would be far better. The modern young man seemed to care for nothing so long as he had enough ,money to keep himself smartly dressed and indulge in the empty pleasures alluded to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>What Teachers and Parents Can Do.</head>
        <p>What Teachers and Parents Can Do.</p>
        <p>It should be the great aim of tho teacher and the parent to help the children to steer clear of becoming cynical, by warning them of the sophistries of the world and "against its deceptions. In the long run, the educated man-- and he was using the word in its widest sense -- had the best of life, even from a materialistic .point of view. Parents and 'teachers should do their utmost to inculcate into children a love of education for its own sake. in this way, so much could be done to make the rising genera- tion better than the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>Good Attendances.</head>
        <p>Good Attendances.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon an address was given by the Rev, T. T. James, of the -Wycliffe Congrega- tional Church.. All three services were well attended, and good collections- were taken for the Sunday school funds. The beautiful singing of the children was a prominent feature of the gatherings. Amongst the music rendered in the afternoon were " The Rose Is Queen," God Bless The Little Chil- .dren " (composed by the organist, Mr. .3. M. Muir), "Evening Song" and a solo and chorus "Children's Voices" (solo by Mrs. F. Monks). In the morning the choir gave "There's a Friend For Little Children" (Watson), solo by Miss Mellor. At the evening service the anthem "Teach Me, 0 Lore" (Attwood) was rendered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>MALEDICTORY PSALMS.</head>
        <p>THE MALEDICTORY PSALMS.</p>
        <p>Vicar Of Buriontiood Thinks They Might Be "Sub-Ediied."</p>
        <p>In the current issue of his Parish Magazine the Vicar (Rev. A. M. Mitchell) writes of "Church Maledictions," and asks: "What im- pression is left in the minds of thoughtful and devout worshippers by such lines these: Let them be as dust before the wind. Let their way be dark and slippery, and let the Angel of the Lord -persecute them. Let a sudden destruction come upon him un- awares. Let his day's be few. Let Satan stand at his right hand. Let them fall from one wickedness to another. Let them be wiped out of the book of the living. When he shall be judged let him be condemned. Let his children be fatherless, and his wife a widow. Let his children be vagabonds and beg their bread. Let there be no man to pity him. ..</p>
        <p>The Vicar adds: "How far these particular psalms, or part psalms, are responsible for the harsh, vindictive, unforgiving spirit of so many nominal Christians, is an interesting but inex- plicable point. The effect cannot be helpful; to the thoughtful, it must be harmful, bow harm- ful who can say? Imperceptibly, as the result of familiarity through constant repetition of the imprecations of psalms, the Mind may be permeated with the spirit of malediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>Plea for More Judicious Use of the Psalter.</head>
        <p>Plea for More Judicious Use of the Psalter.</p>
        <p>"The revision of the psalter is not in question, but a-more judicious use of it in public wor- ship is called for. The imprecatory portions may, with much advantage every way, be de- leted from Morning and Evening Prayer. Such deletion would afford intense satisfaction to mul- titudes of simple folk . . . who are pained by being expected to join in the maledictions of ages past—a time when man was still much of the savage and almost whblly.- ignorant of civil- isation and culture."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>"DEEPEST DEPTHS OF DIABOLISM."</head>
        <p>"DEEPEST DEPTHS OF DIABOLISM."</p>
        <p>'Vicar Of Burtonwood On "Incarnate</p>
        <p>Fiends " From Germany.</p>
        <p>Writing under the heading "The Devils!" in the current issue of his Parish Magazine, the Vicar of Burtonwood (the 'Rev. A. M. Mitchell) says:— "That, contrary to all usages of civilised war- fare and in spite of pledges recently given, asphyxiating gas has been used by our German foes in the long-drawn and still raging battle round Ypres, there is now not the shadow of a doubt. The doers of a deed of this kind, what• ever they may style themselves, Chriwitians or Mahomedans, Free-thinkers or Atheists, are not men but fiends incarnate. Let them hence- forth be known and called by the nanle they have so fitly earned by their malignity—the devils. Devils, we presume, can IN in.carnato; they can take to themselves the likehtess of men: Diabolism assumes many forms, resorts to many devices, practises many enormities, but .it is difficult to imagine a deeper depth of diabolism than the use of poisonous gas for the overthrow and destruction of an unsus- pecting and honourable foe. The Blood of the Britisher Fired.</p>
        <p>" Alas! this does not stand alone; the cruel, vindictive, outrageous treatment of British prisoners in German hands is proved on un- deniable evidence to be only too true—too pain- fully true. The blood of every Britisher is up, is at boiling villa: the incarnate fiends, the devils who do these crowning deeds of devilry will pay dearly for their infamy. Sinch cruel. diabolical deeds must be accounted for and reckoned with. In the face of such doings neutrality is hardly possible—" the neutrals )!</p>
        <p>can no longer stand by never lifting voice or hand to stay such desperate, hellish methods for securing a small and temporary advantage.</p>
        <p>" The Fumes of Hell."</p>
        <p>"We may not retaliate—strong as the pro- vocation is we may not do to them as they ha N. e done to us, hut the penalty is sure; they who create the devilish atmosphere and let loose the fumes of hell will find in these their own cer- tain retribution.</p>
        <p>"By the torpedoing of the Lusifania on Friday, May 7th, the deeper depth of the foul, deliberate . murder would appear to have been sounded. What further devil pranks .ea it Prussianism, in its desperation, devise for the gratification of its hellish spite and malignity ? The deeper depth than the wholesale murder of 1,409 innocent, defenceless people, some forty children among- them, must be deep in- deed—Prussianism for hate, cruelty. and bar- barity is unfathomable."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Vicar Of Sirelion On Its Importance.</p>
        <p>The Rev. and Hon. C. F. Cross, vicar of Stream, preaching at Holy Trinity Church, Warrington, on Sunday morning, on behalf of the Sunday schools of the parish, took for his text; "Fear not, little flock, for it is your Father's ,good pleasure to give you the king- dom." Sunday schools, he said, were the nur- series of the church; they paved the way for an earnest, useful, Christian ,life. Sunday schools appealed to. Parishioners as a whole, whose prayers and gifts should go to assist, to the best of their ability, the good work being done. All should give or their substance so that the build- ings might be furnished in a way to secure efficiency. Just as all could not fight at .the Front, all could not teach in the school; but there were very few who could not.render valu- able assistance in some way or other. The evening sermon was preached by the Rev. B. E. Taylor, curate-in-charge of All Saints', Newton-le-Willows. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>KING'S LEAD IS " SPLENDID " !</head>
        <p>KING'S LEAD IS " SPLENDID " !</p>
        <p>Temperance Reformers' Great Oppor- tunity, Says Burtonwood's lear.</p>
        <p>" For the war-time, anyhow, the King has become a total abstainer," the Rev. A. M. Mitchell points out in the current issue of his Parish M agazine, and adds :—" Who, a year ago, ever dreamt of such a thing coming to pass? A king as total abstainer and asking the country to follow his example! It seems almost too wonderful to be true, but indeed it is a bright reality. The Royal lead is splendid; are we following where the King has gone before? Such a good, brave, high example is worthy our emulation; to do so is a stronger proof of loyalty. than to shout in church, on platform at meeting, God save the King!' The Cabinet is said to follow where the King has led the way. Now is the great opportunity of the Temperance reformer. 'Press the reform home. Public bodies should show their patriot- ism at " this time in this royal way. All are not too ready to do, so. 'At a recent meeting of our local Board of Guardians the suggestion to follow the King's good example was received with derisive laughter. At the same meeting another publican was added to the Assessment Committee—and yet through the Drink we may lose the war."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>IiONA AA Ot 'LD THE APOSTLES FARE TO-DAY?</head>
        <p>IiONA AA Ot 'LD THE APOSTLES FARE TO-DAY?</p>
        <p>The Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, -vicar of. Walton, who preached at the evening service at St. Peter's Church on Sunday—the school anni- versary—said the slow 'growth of the Catholic Church in a land of heathenism was unlike any other story; the first spread of Christianity was a marvellous story. What would be the result now if twelve labouring men went forth from one of our country districts to preach a new religion, that sacred books must be put aside, that old traditions must be rejected, and a new god Worshipped—not to give up worship altogether, but to adopt fresh forms and a fresh object of worship? What 'would he their chance of success? Yet that was what practically happened 1,900 years ago. Everything seemed against the Apostles, yet what the world re- fused to hear the world was forced to hear, and the Gospel was established. They noticed the slow progress the Church seemed to make; they thought that religion was just holding its own, and no more; they saw that comparatively few people w.nt to church ri-gularly in proroi•- tion to the number who stayed away; but they knew that the Divine way was to carry on work by almost imperceptible progress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>WINICK VESTRY.</head>
        <p>WINICK VESTRY.</p>
        <p>Canon Penrhyn presided over the adjourned vestry meeting of St. Oswald's Church, Win- wick, on Friday of last week. The church- wardens' accounts showed a credit balance of £2 Bs. lid. The sum of £53 3s. sd. had been devoted to charities and societies outside the parish. The appointment of the following church officials was confirmed: Churchwardens, Messrs. G. I'. Greenway and W. Massey; sidesmen, Messrs. J. Walker, W. Warburton, F. J. Uncles, P. Irving Dutton; vestry clerk, Mr. J. E. Pettener; lay representatives, Messrs. R. Stone and P. Longton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>SHORT SERMONS AND GABBLED</head>
        <p>SHORT SERMONS AND GABBLED</p>
        <p>SERVICE‘;.</p>
        <p>Rev. 11. Alitehell And Craze For</p>
        <p>Wel ily In The Pulpit.</p>
        <p>Asking the question as to *hether church services are too long, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, in the current issue of his Parish Magazine, proceeds to answer it as follows:— Generally speaking it is so, Church services are too long, in the industrial and rural dis- tricts especially. A service one hour and a quarter long is amply sufficient, and should afford time for a sermon of half an hour's duration. The modern craze for short sermons is not good—it should not be encouraged. As a rule the service it is that needs curtailment— not the sermon. Short sermons and gabbled services do not tend to edification nor full con- gregations. Such is the teaching of experience. A shortened service, with more time given to teaching, would prove far more helpful to ordinary congregations, and be productive of more satisfactory results, from the standpoint of .Anglican Churchmanship. The Psalms are too long, the lessons are too long, and when there is an amalgamation of services, say Morning Prayer, followed by Holy Communion (or Ante-Communion), vain repetitions are not wanting. Why say the Lord's Prayer three times over 2 Why, again, 'recite two Creeds? Where music and singing are both deficient; when Psalms and canticles are nothing but a long-drawn drawl, elongated services become a weariness' to soul and body.</p>
        <p>Why Not Select the Psalms? It may reasonably be asked, and is asked, " Why should congregations be expected to go through three long Psalms at any one service, just for the sake of getting through the Psalter in a month's time "Would it not be better to sing with the understanding one Psalm, or two short ones, suitable to the Sunday or the occasion, and in harmony with the Church teaching for the particular day?" The Psalms follow the days of the month, not the teachings of the Epistle and Gospel and other Scriptures appointed for the day. Should they not he chosen with the same care as hymns are chosen?</p>
        <p>The Old Testament lessons are mostly inord- inately long, and, if read. with expression, they Anake a big demand on the service time. Some of them, moreover, as the record of vindictive- ness and slaughter, are not desirable reading in public; the indelicacy which SO often marks them ought not to be inflicted on mixed con- gregations,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>WOMAN'S WORLD.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Need for Economy.</p>
        <p>The food supply of these islands may, in the near future, become a very serious ques- tion, and it is incumbent on etery housewife to practise a Wise economy in time. Waste not, want not, and take care of the pence, &amp;c., are two proverbs that should be kept in mind by every man and woman to-day. The abund- ance of food of all kinds in the past has tended to make us a wasteful nation, and insistence on economy in the household has been con- sidered akin to meanness. The war has changed this, as well as many other notions.</p>
        <p>Neglected Foods. Many so-called weeds are edible and were formerly used as food. The tender shoots of the nettle, for instance, are not only appetis- ing, but of great value medicinally. The stems of charlock and wild mustard are excel- lent if boiled. The Japanese eat with great relish the fronds of fhe bracken fern, which resemble asparagus. Sorrel used to be exten- sively eaten. It grows freely, almost every- where, and makes a piquant addition to an ordinary salad. The young leaves of the dandelion and those of the chicory plant are also valuable foods. . The leaves of beetroot are excellent served like spinach, and even flowers may be added • to the list of neglected foods. The blossoms of the nasturtium are by no means out of place in the'salad bowl; the leaves also may. be added, whilst both make appetising and wholesome sandwiches. Instances of waste and neglect of good food that Nature lavishly supplies free of cost might be multiplied indefinitely, to say noth- ing of the thousands of tons of mushrooms which are yearly left to rot on the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>Women As Agriculturists.</head>
        <p>Women As Agriculturists.</p>
        <p>It is gratifying to note that women are everywhere taking up agriculture. The Natidnal Political League is helping to form County Committees of experienced men and women, who will select suitable candidates and train them in the several branches of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>lIOIIE NOTES.</head>
        <p>lIOIIE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful</p>
        <p>Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Eggs an Casserole. Ingredients: 4oz. of grated cheese, 4 eggs, 1 shallot, 11oz. of butter, some crushed grape-nuts, salt, pepper, and chopped parsley. Method: Mince the shallot and fry it lightly in the butter; then pour it into a fireproof dish, and spread about so as to grease the dish well. Sprinkle thickly with cheese and crushed grape- nuts. Break the eggs carefully on to the cheese, sprinkle with pepper and salt, and cover with grated cheese and grape-nuts. Put some small pieces of butter on the top, bake in a moderate oven for about ten or twelve minutes, and serve immediately. Baked Souffle Potatoes.</p>
        <p>Select potatoes which are of the same size. Wash and scrub them thoroughly, mid bake in a very hot oven. As soon as tender, cut the top from each and scoop the potato into a heated bowl. Mash smoothly, adding for each potato half a tablespoonful of butter and one tablespoon ful of cream. , Season lightly, and whip until light. For half a dozen potatoes, whip the whites of two eggs to a stiff, dry froth, cut them lightly into the mixture, and refill the shells. Arrange in a pan, and return to the oven until heated through and a pale brown on top. Rice and Oatmeal Pudding.</p>
        <p>Put a teaspoonful of rice alkd a teaspoonful of coarse oatmeal in a saucepan with enough water to cover all, and simmer till the rice is half cooked. Add 2oz. of chopped suet, some grated cheese, and bake in a pie-dish. Baby's Cot.</p>
        <p>A baby should never be allowed to sleep in the same bed as its mother or _nurse, says Maud, in " Farm and Home?' • There is always the danger of its being overlaid, and it is essential therefore that it should have its own little cot from the very first. A cradle should be provided and baby should sleep in it the greater part of the day. This should not be placed on the floor, bat either on a stand or on two chairs arranged in such a way that their backs prevent it from being pulled over by other children. An ordinary washing basket, a Japanese basket, or empty banana crate all make excellent cradles. Whichever is selected it should be lined with some washing material, this keeps out any draughts, and helps to maintain the warmth of the child. The mattress should be fairly thick and stuffed with fine hail; a frequent cause of chilliness is too thin a. mattress or too little clothing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>4.4.4.4.4.,t:.+++++++++++++4•••:. + + t • 4. v Children' sCorner.t ee ...</head>
        <p>4.4.4.4.4.,t:.+++++++++++++4•••:. + + t • 4. v Children' sCorner.t ee ...</p>
        <p>+ Conducted by UNCLE NED. 4.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN OF ALL ACED are cordially invited to Join this Corner if they read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to "UNCLE NED, 'Examiner' Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held every week, to the winners of which useful book-prizes are sent.</p>
        <p>MORE NEW COMPETITIONS.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR BOIS AND GIRLS,-</p>
        <p>You all know, don't you? the title of our capital competition which closes on Monday. " lify Favourite Book I". Only a few lines want! Nothing could be simpler. I can tell that this little essay is going to be a favourite,' because I / actually received an entry a whole week ago! Of course there isn't a single, one of my nephews and nieces but has a favourite book. And that which we like better than. anything relse—what could be easier to write about?</p>
        <p>Remember, then! boys and girls all. A tip- top entry, if you please!</p>
        <p>You Take Your Choice.</p>
        <p>A week on Monday closes our next corn- paition, and in this one I am goiLg to let you please yourself to a large extent. The Prize will be given for the best few lines upon " Kitchener " or " Jellicoe " or French." Only write about one, whatever you do; and please yourself which. [I myself am jolly glad I have not to choose—because for the life of me I couldn't say which I like bea or admire most!, So you will have an interesting task.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>Summer Flounced Dress</head>
        <p>The Summer Flounced Dress</p>
        <p>The advent of the summer flounced frock will be hailed with delight by the.young girl, but it can also be arranged to suit women of all ages. Flounces of white darned net, lace, or embroidered lawn, chiffon and ninon are the best adapted for the youthful, whilst those of lace, in black, silvery white, dull gold, and other shades, are more suitable for the elderly. One very deep flounce starting from the waist, and reaching below the knee, with a narrower one below it, is as modish as the skirt con- sisting of three or more flounces of equal depth. Sometimes- the flounces of more sub- stantial materials such as baptiste are battle- mented, and as many as four of these appeared on one model. A jacket of taffetas, with dainty waistcoat, may be worn with this type of skirt; the corsage is -studiously simple, and very often picturesque. The upper one of two flounces is generally •wired invisibly, to give a slight crinoline effect. -White lace flounces are often made up on a coloured silk foundation, which shows through.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>Vogue of the Coatee.</head>
        <p>The Vogue of the Coatee.</p>
        <p>Coatees vie with collars in variety of design anAl material. Russia provided the original idea of the loose coatee, which lends itself so admirably ,to summer materials, such as voiles, crepons, linens and niiion. Zouave boleros in taffetas gain in favour every day, for they are very original, and altogether feminine. They form an attractive accessory to the afternoon gown, and in many instances are sleeveless. The loose coatee is undoubtedly the most suitable garment for wearing with the full short skirt. It is often scalloped and braided, but no amateur should attempt either performance. A novel coatee of the Moyen Age type has a rucked front covering the hips, add a sash with ends falling in front. A very pretty high collar, quite one of the newest to be worn with a coatee or blouse, was finished at the back with a bow of ribbon, from which depended long streamers reaching to the waist.</p>
        <p>under the child. The pillow should be well filled with down and fairly flat. The sheets must be of soft fine twill or cotton, but never of linen. The blankets must be warm and light. A woollen coverlet gives extra warmth in winter time. A small india-rubber hot water bottle covered in a flannel jacket is of great use, and helps to maintain warmth. A square of mackintosh should be used under the bottom sheet or blanket, but this should never be necessary after the first few iteeks, except in cases of illness. Tiny babies should at first sleep in blankets. Everything should be thoroughly warmed before the baby is put into its cot; this prevents him getting cold and ensures a sound sleep.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>lIERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>lIERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Knowledge is—an Excuse.</p>
        <p>"So you are going to teach your daughter</p>
        <p>music?" "Yes," answered Mrs. Thingilt, "just enough to give us an excuse for having a piano lamp and a mahogany music rack."</p>
        <p>Logical Deduction. Ella: " Maude says Tom Proposed to her, but they are not engaged."</p>
        <p>Clare: " Part-of that statement is false. 11 he proposed they are engaged; if they are not engaged he didn't propose."</p>
        <p>A Modern Girl.</p>
        <p>" Did you break this vase, Priscilla?"</p>
        <p>" It would be somewhat at variance with the truth, madam, personally to assume the fracture. The vase slipped from my fingers. It was the abrupt collision with the floor that caused its disintegration."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>How It Came To Be That Way.</head>
        <p>How It Came To Be That Way.</p>
        <p>Mrs. J.'s patience was much tried by a ser- vant who had the habit of standing around with her mouth open. One day as the maid waited upon the table., her mouth was open as usual and her mistress said: " Mary, your mouth is open." , " Yassum," replied Mary; "I opened it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>Shawls and Togas.</head>
        <p>Shawls and Togas.</p>
        <p>Women in Madagascar drape their ha w 1 as the old Roman senators did their togas. The Roman custom was to wear the toga wrapped around the body aria across one shoulder, leaving the other uncovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>Still More Members.</head>
        <p>Still More Members.</p>
        <p>With the greatest possible pleasure I have received the following splendid note : Glaslyn, Green-lane, l'adgate, 19th May, 1915. Dear Uncle Ned,—My brother and I would like to become members of your Corner. My brother's name is ROT DAKIN. I hope all your other nephews and nieces are all very well.—Yours truly, CHARLES DAKIN.</p>
        <p>With gladness I note your wishes, Charles and Roy; and with the same enrol you as mem- bers of the Children's Corner. Very soon I hope to see your names on our competition lists.</p>
        <p>With all good wishes from Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>The lambs play always; they know no better; They are only one times one. —JEAN INGELOW.</p>
        <p>FIRST IMPRESSIONS.</p>
        <p>" What did you learn at school to-day, dearie " asked the mother of little Mabel when the child returned from her first day at school.</p>
        <p>" I didn't learn anything." was Mabel's dis- gusted reply. "There was a woman there that didn't know a single thing. I had to tell her everything:'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>" THORPE'S CORPSE."</head>
        <p>" THORPE'S CORPSE."</p>
        <p>Miser Thorpe's brother died, and left the miser a fortune on condition that he put up over him a £2O tombstone suitably inscribed. The miser ordered the £2O tombstone—there was no getting round that—but he was deter- mined to save on the inscription. The stonecutter proposed— Here lies the corp Of Joseph Thorpe.</p>
        <p>But this the miser refused, us too long. He cut it down till it read—</p>
        <p>'lllurrpeN Corpse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>Charming Millinery.</head>
        <p>Charming Millinery.</p>
        <p>There is no denying the charm of this season's millinery, inspired for the most part by the modes prevalent in Early Victorian days. Hats may be divided into two classes, the wide- brimmed and the very small brimless shapes that fit close down on the hair. The decora- tions of the former are flat, a single feather or mount completing the trimming; the latter have a wreath round the crown, or a cluster of flowers in front and generally a floating veil behind. White felt hats will be as popular this summer as last; they are to be had in the prettiest shades, and are very simply trimmed. Dunstable straws have come back again, and in large sailors of the Spanish type, have most gracefully curved brims. Some small hats have no trimming at all, unless the two enormous pins, placed on either side, may be so desig- nated. Fashionable Jewellery.</p>
        <p>Rubies, emeralds and diamonds are not now considered in good taste. Leaders of fashion have laid them aside for the present, and the semi-precious stones are worn in their place. The deep blue ameythists of Achil Island, and the sea-green aquamarine, of Kerry, are much in evidence. Green gems are greatly favoured, and many women are wearing olivines, peridots and tourmalines.. Topazes aml zercons—the latter are brownish yellow in colour—are also sought after. Green jade bracelets are very fashionable, and much pink coral is seen. Garnets too, have been. revited. They are very handsome stones, and were greatly prized by our grandmothers. Those people who possess the sets formerly displayed in Continental watering places limy just now consider them- selves lucky.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>Patron Saint of London.</head>
        <p>The Patron Saint of London.</p>
        <p>That London has a patron saint is a fact, of which most Londoners are ignorant, nor is his name even known, or familiar .to the inhabi- tants of the Metropolis. The /first Christian Cathedral in London was erected on the site of the Roman camp and altar to Diana, and St. Mellitus was its first bishop. As the patron saint of the City of London his name has lately been placed on a new banner, borne in processions on festival days in St. Paul's, and thus interest has been aroused in this bishop who lived in Saxon times. King Ethelbert, granted certain lands in Essex to the bishop, for the maintenance of the cathedral.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>HINTs ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTs ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information in Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>The French Peasant and Effects of Drink.</p>
        <p>A French (lector driven from his region of the North of France had to find a practice else- where, so he went West and is now practising as a country doctor. He found that the farmers and peasants had learnt how to distil spirits from any kind of vegetable matter, such as rotten potatoes, and the consequences were sometimes deplorable. The still was never idle, not even on Sundays. Every hoase had its own still. The war hindered it not at all. The smallest establishment gets 40 or 50 litres of potato eau de vie. jt was on offer everywhere, and no occasion to drink was neglected. The drinkers were unconscious of the, danger in- volved in its use. Ilpilepsy was one conse- quence, tuberculosis another; it was rare to see an aged person in that region. Suicides, violent deaths, accidents of all sorts that go with alco- holism were of daily occurrence. Influenza, else), easily complicated with pneumonia, is nearly always fatal to the hard drinker.. The doctor presages a speedy end to the race, once so frugal and industrious, unless the " powers " interfere to deprive these peasants,of their un- fortunate privilege of distilling their own poison. The Effect of Environment.</p>
        <p>The Hampstead Garden Suburb is proving a desirable place of residence for children, so far as their health is concerned. Dr. F. W. Andrew, medical officer of health for Hendon—in which urban district the suburb is included—gives in his last annual report the results of his inspec- tion of the children in the elementary schools of the district. They prove the same tendency that has been. noted at Port Sunlight and at Bournville, for the children brought up amid natural conditions to be sturdier of limb and greater in stature than those who dwell in ordinary districts. Other factors than housing doubtless enter into the comparison, but the following figures with regard to the heights of boys in the garden suburb school and the over- age for all the schools in the district of Hendon is significant _</p>
        <p>5-6 yrs. 6-7 yys. 7-8 yrs. ft. in. ft. in. ft. in. Garden suburb school 3 7 3 91 3 lit Hendon schools .</p>
        <p>In the case of girls there is a similar increase of about one inch in height at each series of years; with regard to weight equally .satisfac- tory results are observed.—". The Medical Officer."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>NO SCARECROWS NEEDED.</head>
        <p>NO SCARECROWS NEEDED.</p>
        <p>A farmer recently paid a visit to a neigh- bour, and as he passed along by the side of the fields he made a mental note of the fact that no scarecrows were visible. Meeting his neighbour almost immediately, he opened the conversation as follows :</p>
        <p>"Good morning, Mr. Oates. I see you have no scarecrows in your field. How do you manage without them?" "Oh! well enough," was the innocent reply. "You see I don't need 'em, for I'm in the field all day myself."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>JOHNNY'S KINDNESS,</head>
        <p>JOHNNY'S KINDNESS,</p>
        <p>I found him in the nursery This afternoon at two. The tears were rolling woefully From out his eyes of blue. He looked at me with angry frown, And cried in peevish tone, "Mother and Maud have gone to town, And left me here alone." "But, Johnny, dear," I kindly said, "You're very small to take; 'Come, share with me .a feast instead Of plum and currant cake!" But darker grew poor Johnny's gloom. He looked at me and said, "Make sister Maudie stay at home, And let me go instead. They only took me yesterday." (He •rubbed his tearful brow.) "Oh, take that nasty cake away! I do not want it now." He pushed aside the plums and all, However sweet and brown, - • And louder still I heard him bawl, " I want to go to town." But ere the clock was striking three I had a caller shy, He'd come to make it up with. me, And all his tears were dry. 'Twos not the cake that made him come, For Jack was not a fraud; And, by his orders, every crumb Was set aside for/ Maud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>Revelation.</head>
        <p>A Revelation.</p>
        <p>The delicious crispness, freedom from all trace of greasiness, and the perfect digestibility, only obtainable when ATORA Beef Suet is used for frying fish and pancakes, is a revelation. Ask your grocer for .ATORA IN /mom and refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>DEMOCRACY AND WAR.</head>
        <p>DEMOCRACY AND WAR.</p>
        <p>New Union To Provide For International Peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>SOME NOVEL PROPOSALS.</head>
        <p>SOME NOVEL PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>I.—No province shall be transferred from one Government to another without the consent by plebiscite or otherwise, of the population of such province. 2.—No treaty, arrangement or undertaking shall be entered upon in the name of Great Britain without the sanction of Parliament. Adequate machinery for ensuring democratic control of foreign policy shall be created. 3.—The foreign policy of Great Britain shall not be aimed at creating alliances for the pur- pose of maintaining the balance of power, but shall be directed to concerted action between the Powers, and the setting up of an Inter- national Council whose deliberations and decisions shall be public, with such machinery for securing international agreement as shall be the guarantee of an abiding peace. 4.—Great Britain shall propose as part of the peace settlement a plan for the drastic reduc- tion, by consent, of the armaments of all the belligerent Powers, and to facilitate that policy shall attempt to secure the general nationalisa- tion of the manufacture of armaments, and the control of the export of armaments by one country to another.</p>
        <p>These are the four cardinal points in the policy of a new movement—the Union of Demo- cratic. Control—the inauguration of which is an outcome of the war. Under the auspices of the Warrington branch, a well-attended and animated- inectin; was, recently helti in the Friends' Meeting house, Buttermarket-street. Mr. J. A. Ransome (president) was in ;the chair, and an address was given by the Rev. Dfinnico (late of Warrington). Mr. H. Sherburn (secretary) was also present. The policy of the movement was discussed at length.</p>
        <p>Nlll. CIIFIELD :AND GERMANY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>Democratic Control Of International</head>
        <p>Democratic Control Of International</p>
        <p>Affairs.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Cros4eld, who had received an invitation to attend, wrote a letter to Mr. Hey-wood explaining that it was impossible for him to be present, but expressing the hope that the following points might be emphasised in the speeches :- 1. That all Prussian militarism should be crushed, but no patched-up peace should be tolerated, but that the Allied Powers, which, after all, stand not only for justice to the smaller nations of Europe, and their protec- tion, but for civilisation and progress, should vindicate these great causes and establish their authority in such a way as to make it for. ever impossible for Germany and Austria to repeat so horrible a crime against humanity.</p>
        <p>2. That the nations- who are guifiy: of this colossal crime should be punished for it, aml compelled to make such partial reparation as alone is possible, first to lielgium and next to others of the Allies.</p>
        <p>3. That while punishment must be inflicted and reparation made, these nations should yet have their opportunity, in the course of gener- ations to come, to expiate their crime and atone for it so that in the future, under the wiser guidance of democratic rule and influ- ence, and changed in mind and spirit, they may yet be regenerated, and yet share in the useful and progressive work of civilisation. 4. That the futile attempt to maintain a balance of power in Europe, with all its vicious and fatal consequences, may give place to one powerful alliance, strong enough to guarantee the rights and independent exist- ence. of all nations.</p>
        <p>s..lJiat • with a view to this being carried into effect the minciples of nationality would to the fullest extent that is practicable and possible be recognised, when the time comes to arrange the conditions of peace—a time when to no small extent it will certainly be necessary to re-draw the map of Europe. 6. If this policy is followed it will follow from it that secret diplomacy and secret nego- tiations would to a large extent disappear, while secret international treaties would be abolished altogether, democratic government exercising an amount of authority and control in foreign affairs beyond anything hitherto known in our political history, and in the political history of Europe. ORIGIN OF THE MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that, to those men who had been working for good-feeling among. the nations, the war came as a terrible catas- trophe; and some of them—all very prominent and influential persons—decided to start this new movement. Owing to the condition of things; and the public .feeling, they had thought it wise to organise quietly—which had been ,well done in the various towns during the winter. Mr. liansome then read out the objects, and asked for discussion thereon. A Member: How would you propose to get the, other Powers to agree to this? (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Plinston: The principle of democratic control is that the people rule. If democratic control is obtained, the people can elect repre- sentation to the other Powers. Of course, after that was declared we should have to trult to their honour. That is the only way. I think the peoples of all the other Powers are satisfied in their bitn minds that war must not even be mentioned again, and the people will be in a large majority.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Now naiion can really want war. We went into it because we were bound by treaties.</p>
        <p>THE CALL FOR EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Plinston : War brings out all the beast- lusts of a, people. How then are you going to get that ideal state? Only by education! It is the duty of us who are not taking part in the conilict to educate the people towards that end.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: That is exactly the idea of the 'Union. There is no doubt that the more we can get the peoples of all countries to think, the sooner we shall put an end to war. 'HOW DEMOCRACY WILL GAIN POWER. Rev. H. T. Dixon (of Penketh) asked how they were going to get democratic control? What he was most concerned .about was that democracy was becoming . less and less to them. England never had been a democratic country, and they had scarcely a shred of real democracy left. The people had abso- lutely no power; and before they could do what was suggested—send a representative and " all that sort of thing "—they would in some way have 'to get in power. Personally he could not see any hope of getting that out of this war, unless the war was followed by a revolution. That was how the people must get in power; and then obtain education and enlightening in their own lands!</p>
        <p>• Mr. W. Hill: Do you think we should be able to get nationalisation of armaments into conformity with the English laws—knowing that Germany hates us, and will hate us more than ever when the war is over? Again, some nations really believe in war as a righteous</p>
        <p>others an have nfidenee</p>
        <p>ord thing, and are never satisfied unless toe.' fighting. . It would take years to do it! The Chairman: Of course, one hardly es,v:. all these things will come about to-morror</p>
        <p>A IC' ALL COUNTRIES MUST BE DEMOC"n"T 08' Mr. Elwell: The nationalisation a °hoe ments is all right if the other nationslA, democratic control. lam in favour international council, but we have hi!" toe example of one at the Hague. What tiler to of an international council without 10'1'</p>
        <p>enforce its decisions?</p>
        <p>_ Mr. James llamblett: Why should Ave '3et at all? We are killing the very thing out for. If we believe in democracy at I"tiot must carry it out to its fullest sense—and</p>
        <p>is, the true spirit of brotherhood. " The Chairman: Of course, nationalisatio only a stepping-stone. Her . tGherayfirssut clause. coMnsii.d. only became possible if enough people,. Me, grasp the point. History was full. P,:\e\'' suggested that This theyr r sohpoous dalkit failure of one nation trying to pzikr upon another. The Allies must renielub't when there came a great movement on theo'ili; of most people to crush Germany and bll.- ate her. We must remember the succ„,itil our own magnanimous experiment with °"-- Africa.</p>
        <p>CAUSES, NOT EFFECTS. 1 11° Mr. J. Haley mentioned that they Union of Democratic Control in Grer°l„"ilio (Hear, Bear.) The four points seemed to deal merely with effects—the effects of diPloraslo and policy. • But there was cause. TiMei 1110 time again we had engaged in wars in whicl,l,o• heart of the people was not. We were now ing—there was not the slightest doubt—a!"rest talistic war; and when commercial jlj came in, they could depend that moral to, ciple went out. They had to fight the c7...4 not the effect. (Hear, bear.) d The Chairman said they could not Pu- t as the cause one item. •ors 7</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Hawthorn said he believed the .I:viiot of this democratic union and the See of movement was entirely wasted. All ser".ol remedies were suggested, but they had o°,oo scrap of democratic- power. It was in 11,117d0 nature to wage wars, and they would 40,01 continue—between nations, indiFiduals, eiti-the and labour; the strong would alivays a#o c'o e ; weak.—At the same time, he had oble:00 to helping to lift up human nature and foil! tbo properly-directed education. Let them give4l. parents the right training to bring up children, insthad of trying to do the impose</p>
        <p>REVOLUTION. t Mr. Plinston asked if Mr. Dixon, by " lution," had meant revolution of thought' piA Mr. Dixon: ,By revolution I mean forces', t necessarily those of bloodshed or war. J3ll4ild the people are to get back their power i'sfe must not only think but act as well. They ',lay. to get back their power; there is no other 1%</p>
        <p>NOT "STOP THE WAR!" di° The Rev. IL Dunnico, in the course of bi 6 -„tie address, stated that the Union of Denlocno Control was not a pro-German movenienvtd: had been stated in some quarters), or it,9106 the-war movement. The war was on, an(2l 010 to be carried through, and the object O' to Union was to make it impossible for IcBr' take place in the future. peret This conflict had been mainly caused by 6- ,fe</p>
        <p>diplomacy, secret service aml secret arlll,ltlP meats. There should be a democratic et pe of foreign policy. Under the present 5y5t,";10 one could enter the diplomatic service rel; 4ad a guaranteed private income of A*" of annum, which limited service to one elB,6sd society. Ability rather than position shoo', acknowledged, and the result wquld be 013' should get better men. (Applause.) jor. It may .be pointed out that the General Ct:;00 cil of the Union passed the following reso.„,lll`o.tv on February 9th : " That the Union of ye ist cratic Control, convinced that democracy 1131:11J be based on the equal citizenship of veil 0 women, invites the co-operation of wonlell'_,d</p>
