      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB BOWLING M</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB BOWLING M</p>
        <p>The first monthly bowling main in connection with the Liberal Club was held on Saturday, and proved very suecessfnl. The prize-winners were: 1, E. Lunt; 2, J. Lawless; 3, T. O'Brien; 4, W. Bibby. The game between Thomas Shaw and Wm. Bibby fot the fourth prize was very ,close. They tied at 14 all, and it fell to Bibby to get in _</p>
        <p>the winting wood. Also the game between T. O'Brien and J. Lawless was ken, Lawless just getting in the winning wood. The finalists were E. Lunt and J. Lawless. The former played a good game, and finished with the score of 15 to 10. The arrangements for the main were car- ried out by Messrs. Thomas Shaw, J. Collins and G. Marshall (secretary of the bowling section of tie club).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <head>WAR FUND HANDICAP. Last Day For Entries.</head>
        <p>WAR FUND HANDICAP. Last Day For Entries.</p>
        <p>To-day is the last day for receiving entries in the big War Fund Bowling Handicap, which is being promoted by the Warrington Works' Amateur Bowling Association, and which gives every promise of being a great success. All amateur bowlers in the town are eligible to enter upon payment of a shilling fee, and the first prize, £5 55., has been generously given by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, whose keen interest in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>COUNTY HONOURS FOR 'WARRINGTONIANS..</head>
        <p>COUNTY HONOURS FOR 'WARRINGTONIANS..</p>
        <p>Two Warrington bowlers have been honoured by inclusion in the Lancashire team, which is to meet Derbyshire on June sth. They are Messrs. T. Humphreys and W. J. Woods, and they are bah members of the Warrington Ceme- tery team. The county watch will take place at New Mills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>LOCAL BOWLERS WHO HAVE "JOINED UP."</head>
        <p>LOCAL BOWLERS WHO HAVE "JOINED UP."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Bowling Clubs have contri- buted many a man to the ranks of the gallant Army who are upholding the national prestige on the field of war. From the Bull's Head</p>
        <p>Club have gone :—W. Daniels, G. Hackett, J. Massey, H. Fox, W. Andrews, G. Shaw (No. 2), L. Hockenhall, H. Hockenhall, H. Rowley, H. Williams, J. Bate, E. Lunt, J. Simister, and C. Regan. Regan, as already reported in our columns, has been wounded, as, also, has Lunt, whilst Simister is missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET UNITARIANS V. PEN KETH FRIENDS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Unitarians were the visitors at Penketh on Saturday, and proved far too good for the School team, winning by 80 runs. For the win- ners, Cook (39) and Mather (36) made a splendid stand, and putt on 71 runs for the fourth wicket, before Cook was caught. D. Pfinston had another good day with the</p>
        <p>ball, taking seven wickets fo-r eight runs. - Ells- more also bowled well, his three wickets being taken at a cost of nine runs. For Penketh, Mr. Baker took five wickets at a cost of 40 runs. Scores :</p>
        <p>Cairo-street U nitarians.—D. Plinston run out 3, S. Hayes b Mr. Baker 9, Rev. I. Lister c Sed- don b Mr. Baker 2; A. H. Mather b Mr. Baker 36, C. H. Cook c Mr. Brown b Mr. Baker 39,1 G. Knowles 13 Jones 1, A. Ellsmore c Roberts b Jones 1, F. McKie b Mr. Baker 0, R. Chatterton not out 3, W. Hankinson c Ellis-b Mr. Brown 3, J. Moore c Roberts b Mr. Brown 0, extras 5; total 102. Penketh Friends' School—Mr. Baker b Plin- ston 0, Mr. Brungel b Plinston 4, Jones c Ells- more b Plinston 0, Holcroft b Plinston 0, Ellis c Chatterton b Plinston 2, Mr. Brown b Ells- mom 1, Lumb b Plinston 9, Roberts c and b Ellsmore 1, Seddon b Ellsmore 0, Atkinson -b Plinston 0, Ransome not out 0, extras 5; total 22.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD-AVENUE v. WIDNES P.M.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes on Saturday. Scores:--</p>
        <p>Widnes P.M.—A. Cartwright, c Lewis b Dale 1, S. Smith c Hazledene b Phcenix 3, E. Mart- ley c Phcenix b Dale 9, A. Beatty b Phcenix 5, H. Davies 'run out 5, P. Whingates c. Lewis 1) Dale 1, E. Fleet c and b Phcenix 2. J. Baker b Phcenix 0, A. Wiles run out 1, A. Rawlinson b Phcenix 3, J. Shingler not out 1, extras 0; total 31. Elmwood-avenue.---W. Littlewood lbw b Fleet 0, J. Phcenix c Rawlinson b Martley 3, F. H:azledene run out 1, J. Lewis c and.b Fleet 0, H. Dale b Martley 2. J. Molynbux c Beatty b Fleet 0, T. Wells st Davies b Fleet 0, H. Main not out 0, W. Finch c Fleet 13 Martley 5, W. R. Farrar b Martley 0, W. Gibson st Davies b Martley 0, extras 3; total 14.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>SHOOTING MATCH.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING MATCH.</p>
        <p>COMPETITIVE RIFLEMEN.</p>
        <p>Monks, Hill Men Beat Rylangs's</p>
        <p>Team.</p>
        <p>Teams representing the rifle clubs in connec- tion with Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s and Messrs. Rylanilb's works met in friendly rivalry on Wednesday evening. The scores were as follows :</p>
        <p>Rylands's.—R. Allen 88, E. Balmer 87, H. Bate 86, H. Gault 85, S. Williams 83, T. Lowe 77, L. Borley 75, H. Newton'\6B, J. Bramhall 66, Tastin 65---total 780.</p>
        <p>Monks, Hall.—G. Cox 94, A. Webb 93, A. Highley 93, A. Leicester 90, J. Shepley 87, G. Pane 82, J. Sisson 81, W. J. Tilley 78, G. Cote- land 78, J. Rabbit 74—total 850. The result was a win for Monks, Hall squad by 70. The two lowest scorers—W. Houghton (Monks, Hall) 69, and W. Harrison ,(Rylands's), 45—were counted out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>ASSOCIATION PLAYERS</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION PLAYERS</p>
        <p>" Join Up."</p>
        <p>On Monday, Denny Vernon and Percy- Chal- linor, two of the most popular junior players and members of the St. Barnabas' Football Club, along with another Fairfield Athletic footballer, Bate, joined the Royal Fusiliers and left Warrington Central Station by the 11.30 train en route for I,kndon. Several of their playing comrades accompanied them to the station to watch them off, and gave hearty cheers as the train departed from the plat- form. There are now twelve members' of the St. Barnabas' Football Club ih the ranks. amongst whom, unfortunately, Spruce and Hunt have lately been renorted as wounded. Of the Fairfield iithletic Football Club there are eight other members serving at the present time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>BRITISH COLUMBIA FOR THE SPORTSMAN.</head>
        <p>BRITISH COLUMBIA FOR THE SPORTSMAN.</p>
        <p>British Columbia is called the "Sportsman's I Paradise." In this province is to be found a larger variety of game and fish than-in any other part of the continent. Grizzly bears i roam in the interior fastnesses, black bears can be found in all parts of the province, big- horn sheep, goat and caribou abound in the mountain regions, while deer of several varie- ties are to be found all over the province. Elk shooting may be had on the northern portion of Vancouver Island, while panthers are bagged on the coast and in the interior. Of birds there are five species of grouse, all kinds of wild fowl, and on the coast the pheasant affords splendid wing shooting. Fishing is equally good, salmon and trout being the prin- cipal objects of the sportsman's skill. The former are caught with spoon bait in the salt water, as British Columbia salmon do not %gaily rise te the fly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>NO PROFESSIONAL FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>NO PROFESSIONAL FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington Club Committee's War-Time Decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>£3OO DEFICIT ON LAST SEASON.</head>
        <p>£3OO DEFICIT ON LAST SEASON.</p>
        <p>I am sure that it is the unanimous wish of every member of the club that we should not, so long as the war lasts, engage in professional football So far as our committee are concerned, we have passed a resolution that no matter what may take place in the Northern Union, we will not be a party to professional football so long as the war lasts.</p>
        <p>The foregoing statement—perhaps the most important from the point of view of public in- terest—was made by Councillor E. Osborne, the president 'of the Warrington rootball Club, at the annual meeting of that body, which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Thursday evening, and which, contrary to the usual experience, was very sparsely attended by the members, though the Chairman was sup- ported on the platform by the majority of the members of the committee and by other officials, including the Hon. Secretary (Mr. T. P. Brad- shaw) and the Hon. Treasurer (Mr. T. Pem- berton). The President, alluding to the unwontedly small attendance, said that it seemed as if the club had almost as much difficulty in raising a quorum as they had in raising a team, a remark which was received with laughter. Con- tinuing, he said that they met under circum- stances that evening that no one could possibly have anticipated at the last annual meeting of the club. The season just ended had been a most trying one for all concerned, and if it had not been for the loyalty of officials, committee, members and players he was sure they would have been in a much worse condition than the reports showed. Judging by the statements that had been made to him by the various representatives, he thought they would compare very favourably—financially at any rate—with most other clubs. Their position in the League Table wa,s not so good as last year, but when they sonsidered that, on many occasions the number of players had only just been sufficient to make up a team, owing to the players join- ing the Colours, they were not downhearted, but had reason to rejoice in the fact.</p>
        <p>would be able to build up a team that should be capable of beating the best team in the Northern Union.</p>
        <p>HONORARIA "AS USUAL "</p>
        <p>It was decided to vote the officials the usual honoraria, viz., secretary 05; treasurer .05, and auditors 0 eaeh.</p>
        <p>- AND SUBSCRIPTIONS WANTED "AS USUAL"</p>
        <p>Mr. T. England proposed a vote of thanks to the committee for, their services during the past yearl and hoped that next year, the mem- bers wcArld be able to meet under happier auspices, and that the club would go on in a successful manner. He further hoped that none of the members would neglect to pay their sub- scriptions because of the altered conditions, but that they would loyally support the club. Mr. G. Wright, in seconding, thought the members ought to be as loyal to the club as the players who had gone to the Front had been to the country and should pay their subscrip- tion even if there was football or not. The President said that for a few months, at any rate during the beginning of the season, it might be hard on some members to continue their subscription, but he could assure them that if they came forward and gave them the same support as in the past, the committee would deal with the sum collected as carefully as possible. The money would not be squandered, and the matter was one which rested entirely with the members themselves. He hoped that they would come forward as one man and say "There's no football, but you need ray money and there it is !" On the other hand, if some of the members were hard hit, the coin- mittee did not want them to feel that they were out of it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton said that it had been a very anxious time for the committee and officials, as well as a very disheartening time, but they had gone on with their work in very much the same manner as they would have done had the times been normal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>Finances Better Than Expected.</head>
        <p>Finances Better Than Expected.</p>
        <p>Financially, they had come out much better than was expected. The committee had anticipated a loss and set themselves quite early in the season to make as little .as pos- sible. By caiefully watching the pence they had made it very much less than at one time looked possible. Wages, refreshments, clothing. transfer fees, field expenses, printing, &amp;c., were all considerably reduced. Of course, en the other side, the gate receipts and subscriptions were very much down also. The loss for the year as it appeared in the accounts was 2.521 10s-. 9d. That was after allowing for deprecia- tion of property £127 16s. 6d., Intermediate League £65 is., sd. and Schoolboys' League £l7 7s. id., a total 'of £2lO ss. The sum of £lO had since been received. The accounts would show a net loss of £3OO, which, considering all things, was a very creditable state of affairs. He moved the adoption of the reports, and con- gratulated the secretary and treasurer upon the very clear arid concise manner in which they had presented them to the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <head>"THE BRIGHTEST SPOT IN THE REPORT.</head>
        <p>"THE BRIGHTEST SPOT IN THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. England, in seconding, said that that was the first time in the history of the club that he was thankful that they had not won the cup or the League championship; he be- lieved that it would have been no honour and glory to have done so. To his mind the brightest spot in the report was that so many members, players and otherwise, had enlisted and gone to the Front, and in that connection</p>
        <p>he felt sure that such men as Skelhorne, Naylor and Fish would give excellent accounts of themselves. (Applause.)_</p>
        <p>The proposition was carried</p>
        <p>THE OFFICERS RE-ELECTED EN BLOC. It was announced that the officers for the ensuing year bad been re-elected en bloc, as follows:—President, Councillor E. Osborne; lion. secretary, Mr. T. P. Bradshaw; hon. treasurer. Mr. T. Pemberton; committee, Messrs. F. Barber. T. Bunch. T. Barnes, J. F. Foster, W. Hill, W. Mason. J. T. Sampson and J.. Winstanley; auditors, Messrs.' W. Webster and George Gillin; trustees, Messrs. T. Locker and F. Barber.</p>
        <p>PROOF OF THE MEMBERS' APPRECIATION. The President said that the fact that there had not been a single new nomination for any of the positions was in itself a proof of the appreciation of the members of the services which the committee and other officers had rendered during a very trying time. When things became normal again no doubt they would have the usual heated competition for places, and critics on the floor of the house would be as eager as ever to " put`the officials through it." (Laughter.) Well; the sooner such times came the better would the officers appreciate it. That night they felt that they were on different ground altogether and, whilst they realised that it was an honour to be re- elected, yet at the same time they realised that that re-election was due very largely to the state of affairs which the country was in at the pre- sent time. As to the programme for next season, he did not know whether they had all seen the newspaper with regard to racing in war time, but if they had they would have seen that the Government had asked for the general suspension of racing in Great Britain after the present week until the war was ove, and that the stewards of the Jockey Club, without a moment's hesitation, had complied with the request and, from Saturday, would not sanction any raaing meetings with the- solitary exception. of those at Newmarket.</p>
        <p>No Professional Football in War Time. What was the position with regard to pro- fessional football? He was sure it was the unanimous wish of every member of the club that, so long as the war lasted, they should not engage in professional football. What had been suggested and would probably be brought about was that they should encourage the Schoolboys and Intermediate Leagues. If any football was played it should be played by them and not by men who were considered to .be fit for active. service. (Hear, hear.) This matter would be fully discussed at the annual meeting of the Northern Union, but he might say that from, the expressions of opinion he had heard with regard to it that the consensus of opinion seemed to be that they would be doing what was right, fair and honest to the fellows who had gone abroad by suspending professionalism during the continuation of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>Warrington Committee Against War-Time</head>
        <p>Warrington Committee Against War-Time</p>
        <p>Professionalism.</p>
        <p>• So far as the Warrington committee were con- cerned, they had passed a .resolution that no matter..what might take place in the Northern Union they would not be a party to professional football so long as the war lasted. (Applause.) They, were quite determined about that.. One /natter Which. ..concerned them was the financial side of the question. Practically they would be. compelled to provide for about £3OO to meet rent, rates and taxes, the groundsmen's wages, cost of painting, &amp;c. They had already in the bank £240, and they had a further £lO which. they had received from the League, making altogether £250, so that if no football took place. they would have to meet a liability of. probably, £5O. Well, that was not very much, and he was sure that the members of the War- rington Football Club would show their loyalty and that if it was found necessary to find that £5O they 'would give something towards it. (Hear, hear.) If it was found necessorY to fix a levy to raisq that £so—he did 'not know that it would be necessary—it would only be a small matter per, head, seeing that they had nearly a thousand Members.</p>
        <p>When the Boys Come Horn-,‘ Again.</p>
        <p>They hoped 'that they would soon be able to welcome back the players who had gone to fight the country's battles, also the many members who were likewise doing their share, and that when .that happy re-union Wok place they</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>• Troubles The Future May Have hi</p>
        <p>Store.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union League will hold their annual meeting ia June, and in the meantime the question as to die advisability of proceed- ing with the game next season is being freely discussed. There is a general disposition not to prejudice the situation one way or the 'other by making premature resolutions, and it is prob- able an open mind- will be maintained until late in the summer, wheu. the actual state of things on the eve of the season will be tolerably well known.</p>
        <p>Many serious points to be borne in mind in- clude the following: That if football is sus- pended the rents of the grounds will still have to be met; that many players have and will continue to join the colours; that if football is continued the support will be less even than during last season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <head>Grants to Necessitous Clubs.</head>
        <p>Grants to Necessitous Clubs.</p>
        <p>The Northern Rugby League Committee met in Manchester on Tuesday, when tile treasurer reported that the Relief Fund had received the um of -QlB5 9s. id., which represented the con- tributions from players who had received their wages in full.</p>
        <p>The committee decided to make grants to that amount to those clubs whose players had accepted substantial reductions of wages for dis- tribution amongst their players in proportion to the sacrifices they had made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>Claim Not a Good One.</head>
        <p>Claim Not a Good One.</p>
        <p>At the subsequent meeting of the Union Com- mittee the Broughton Rangers Club made a claim against the Salford Club for loss of gate receipts on account of their match peing post- poned from Christmas Day to April 17. The committee decided that there were no grounds for a claim for compensation, but allowed the Broughton Club the sum of .R 7 19s. for expenses incurred in the preparation of the first match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0322" n="art0322">
        <head>LIBERAL WORKERTROLL – OF HONOUR.</head>
