      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0339" n="art0339">
        <p>i • .., • •</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 22, 1915.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND AUSTRALIAN EX-PREMIER</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield and Miss Elliadi were present at the luncheon given in the Harcourt Room in the House of Commons this week in honour of the Hon. W. C. Watson, the ex-Labour Prime Minister of Australia. Mr. Watson made an admirable speech on that occasion, and both at the luncheon and at the great and representative gathering in the Guildhall the intense spirit of patriotism shown testified splendidly to Imperial solid- arity and power. While Mrs. Crosfield and Miss Truda Crosfield were busy all day at the meeting of the Women's National Liberal Federation on Wednesday, Mr. Crosfield and Miss Elliadi attended the memorable and very historic gathering in the Guildhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0340" n="art0340">
        <head>ASYLUM AS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>ASYLUM AS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The Winwick County Asylum has been trans- formed into a military hospital,, capable, if necessary, of accommodating no fewer than 3,000 men. Already a large number of patients have been received, included among them being a number of Australians, who received their injuries in the recent fighting in the 'Dar- danelles. The first batch of patients arrived on Friday of last week, and during the week there have been other arrivals, the total num- ber now quartered in the building—now tem- porarily known, by the way, as the Lord Derby Hospital—being about 400.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0341" n="art0341">
        <head>MUNICIPAL EMPLOYES AND WAR SERVICE.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL EMPLOYES AND WAR SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Trades Council To Keep An Eye</p>
        <p>On The Matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Dukes called attention at a meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council to "the inducement tto get all municipal and urban councils to re- lease as many of their employes as possible, so that they may be moved to places where munitions of war are to be produced." Some Councils, he said, were making war service on the part of certain of their employes a matter of coercion without paying regard to the fact that if they were taken from their homes and their regular work, and set to producing war materials, the men "should be compensated to the degree of their loss." The men were being told that their services would be re- quired anywhere at the will of the Govern- ment, and he thought that the least that could be expected was that they should be treated as well as "the khaki dockers," and that men who were called upon for the special service suggested should receive, in addition to their wages, either a soldier's pay or the equivalent of their maintenance whilst divOrced from their homes and routine occu- pations. He also thought that the factories at present turning out the ordinary materials of commerce, but which were capable of adap- tation to the manufacture of war necessities, should be utilised before men were coerced into leaving their homes and their normal occupations to take up special war work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0342" n="art0342">
        <head>No Coercion Locally.</head>
        <p>No Coercion Locally.</p>
        <p>So far nothing had been done locally in this matter of coercing municipal employes, but a census had been taken as to what the men in the employ of the Council could do and as to who, among them, could be spared. Up to the present, however, he had not heard of any men being transferred. The Council, he thought, should insist that men should not be transferred unless they were to receive an equivalent to the loss such transference would entail.</p>
        <p>"Mayor Makes No Bones About It."</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. Hamblett) : The Mayor makes no bones about it. He says he will bring pressure to bear wherever it is possible. Indeed, pressure is being brought to hear in an ingenious manner, And it is up to as to watch this matter, very, very , closely. It was decided that in the event of any stepg being taken locally in the Matter the Exe'cu- tive Committee of the Trades Council should have power to take what steps they might consider necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0343" n="art0343">
        <head>NO COMPULSION.</head>
        <p>NO COMPULSION.</p>
        <p>On making inquiries from the Town Clerk we find that no coercion is taking lace with the Corporation employes. The following circular has been posted in the Corporation Departments :</p>
        <p>Government War Work.</p>
        <p>The Government are urgently in need of men in connection with the output of munitions. All men who are desirous of assisting are 'requested to give in their names at once. All men will be reinstated after the war. J. LYON WHITTLE, Town Clerk.</p>
        <p>In the event of men giving in their names, and it being found possible to release them, notification would be made to the Board of Trade, and the men would be at perfect liberty to acceptor refuse any job offered. Ile had already pointed out to . the Local Government Board the difficulty with reference to the mov- ing of a man, to another town, and it was only right that every man should be compensated to the degree of his loss; but in the case of the Cor- poration employes there wits no compulsion at all, every man was perfec ly free to make his own choice. Under the present system there could be no compulsory transference by the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0344" n="art0344">
        <head>LATE MR. H. R. HOROBIN.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. H. R. HOROBIN.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on Wednesday of Mr. Henry Richard Horobin, of 51, Sankey-street. He was 66 years of age, and had not been in good health for a considerable time, suffering from heart and lung trouble. He came to War- rington over forty years ago, and after serving 38 years in the telegraphic department on Bank Quay Station, retired in 1902. His second son, Mr. Charles Horobin, was recently appointed .water engineer of the city of Lincoln. The youngest son is serving with the 18th Service Battalion of the King's Liverpool Regiment. The deceased leaves four sons and two daugh- ters. The funeral took place at the Cemetery, the service being conducted by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton. The mourners included Messrs. John, Charles, Albert and Arthur Horobin (sons), Mr. John Horobin, Oxford (brother), and Messrs. H. A. Unsworth (Chester), John Skel- ton, R. A. Joseph, T. Chesworth, Hy. R. Gib- son, W. Bates, E. Hesketh, Robert Sutton, and Dr. Naden. Wreaths, &amp;c., were sent by: "Sallie, Albert and Arthur," "John and Beatty," "Charlie and Cissie" (Lincoln), "John and Annie," "Helen" (sister), "Brother John and family" (Oxford), Mr. and Mrs. H. A. Unsworth (Chester), Miss Sallie Isherwood, Miss Eleanor B. Jones, Dr. and Mrs. Charles Renshaw (Sale), Miss Florrie Booth (South- port), Mrs. Marsh and family, Misses Daintith, Mrs. Chesworth and family, James, Fred and Madge Taylor (Manchester), Mr. and Mrs. Baker and family, Mr. and Mrs. Dumbell, Mrs. Gleave, T. and L. Brandreth, Mr. and Mrs. Wallace Ray, Mr. and Mrs. Milligan (Timper- ley), Mr.' and Mrs. T. Speed (Preston Brook), Miss Nellie Lee, Telegraph Staff, Bank Quay Station; "-Elsie, Mabel, Phyllis and Sonny."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0345" n="art0345">
        <head>MR. T. J. RIDGWAVS SON WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>MR. T. J. RIDGWAVS SON WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Local Australian In The Dardanelles.</p>
        <p>News has been received by Mr. and Mrs. T. J. Ridgway, of Lyrum, that their younger son has been wounded in land fighting in the Dar- danelles. The news is communicated by the High Commissioner of Australia. Lieutenant Althorp Ridgway belongs to the Australian contingent—which he has only joined since the outbreak of war. In peace times he holds the highly important position of Assist- ant White House Engineer to the Common- wealth of Australia. He is brother of Captain T. Ridgway, of the Cheshires. It is not yet known how he has been wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0346" n="art0346">
        <head>WELCOME, DRUIDS!</head>
        <p>WELCOME, DRUIDS!</p>
        <p>Noted Friendly Society Visits Warrington Next Week.</p>
        <p>BUSINESS AND PLEASURE.</p>
        <p>Warrington will be honoured next week by a visit from the Order of Druids, who will hold their fifty-seventh annual 'movable' delegation here by way of compliment to our respected townsman and prominent Druid, Mr. Edwin Allen, who has been the „Grand Master of the Order during the year now drawing to a close. About two hundred delegates will attend as representatives of the various districts, and the meetings will take place in Patten Hall, Winmarleigh-street. The Grand Master will preside,supported by the following officers :Vice- chairman, Bro. John Lavin, Vice Grand Master (who will become Grand Master in suc- cession to Bro. Allen), Tunstall, Staffs; Scrutineer, Bro. Albert Wilde, Past Grand Master, Swinton, Rotherham; Grand Treasurer, Bro. R. Verney Clayton, P.G.M., Prestwich Park, Manchester; Grand Secretary, Bro. J. W. Shaw, P.D.G.M., Manchester. Bro. John Bibby, of Arpley-street, Warring- ton, is on 'the Board of Management of the Order. _</p>
        <p>Bros. R. Perdue, D. Treas., Wm. Woods, D.G.M., A. J. Shaw, D.D.G.M., and Jas. Ray, P.D.G.M., will represent the Warrington Equalised District. Bro. Jas. Banner, P.N. G.A., will represent the "Sir Gilbert Greenall " Lodge (Walton).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0347" n="art0347">
        <head>INSURANCE ACT CHANGES.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE ACT CHANGES.</p>
        <p>Scheme For Centralisation In The</p>
        <p>Order.</p>
        <p>In addition to the customary conference busi- ness of the routine character, proposals of an important character will be considered on the initiative of the Board of Management with regard to the centralisation in the Order for the purposes of National Insurance Act administration. These proposals have been instrumental in attracting the attendance of what will be a record number of delegates. - The scheme for centralising in groups has for its object the banding together of certain dis- tricts for mutual assistance, or for "spreading the risks," in dealing with the delicate claims of National Insurance; but it also involves, as a matter of course, an alteration in the proportion of funds allocated to general funds and retained by the branches. It was thought in the early stages of the consideration of the proposals, says the quarterly report of the Board of Management, that a majority of votes of the delegates at the A.M.D. cast in favour of the centralisation scheme, would be binding on all the branches. Regulations have, however, been recently issued by the Insurance Commissioners governing amalgamation and transfer of engagements under the National Insurance Acts, and by virtue of those regulations the Commissioners have intimated that every brartth having 50 or more insured members must be given the .option of deciding for itself whether or not it will transfer its engagements to the Order. Already a number of branches, Including some of the larger ones, have intimated that they are in favour of the proposals, and have passed the prescribed resolution transferring their engage- ments to the Order as from the Ist January next. On the other hand a number of branches have rejected the proposals, or at any rate have not passed the resolution.</p>
        <p>As there is local option on the question it is suggested that those branches which do not at the moment favour the scheme of centralisa- tion should not attempt to prevent the setting up of 'machinery by the Order for the adminis- tration of the National Insurance Acts in respect of those branches that hold different views. Some provision will have to be made without delay for spreading the risks of the branches that have not a sufficient number of members to constitute an average, and the simplest way out of the difficulty is for those branches to transfer their engagements to the Order, and for the A.M.D. to permit them to do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0348" n="art0348">
        <head>Opposition.</head>
        <p>The Opposition.</p>
        <p>No great objection has been raised to the principle of centralisation; indeed it would be difficult to conceive arguments in opposition to proposals that offer greater security to the mem- bers for obtaining the full rate of benefits after the first valuation. What opposition there is appears to be based largely on the question of administration expenses, the pro- portion available for branches being neces- sarily somewhat reduced. In view of the vested interests that have been created, such opposition was, of course, not unexpected, and we are not disposed to regard it as altogether unreason- able. The result of the first valuation, how- ever, may be the means of awakening the mem- bers to a greater sense of their responsibilities, and if this be the case and a more lively in- terest is taken by them in the government of the Society in the future, • then we have no fear as to what their decision will ultimately be. The present constitution of the Order of Druids in common with that of other Societies of a similar type, provides for the branches being almost entirely self-governed. That the time has arrived when some extension or total change in the constitution should be made, at any rate in respect of National Health Insur- ance, in order to spread the risks of individuals over a wider membership, may be readily appreciated by a careful perusal of the report by the Order's actuaries on the last quin- quennial valuation of the Order. In a supplementary report issued by the Board of Management, it is stated that the Board un- hesitatingly aver that a bold step towards cen- tralisation of the business of the society under the National Insurance Act would be in the best interests of its members, who are insured under the Act. The existing branches of the society with their officials and staff assistants would continue to be utilised, but the.V would. under the centralised system. act as agents of the society itself in respect of National Health Insurance, rather than as separate entities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0349" n="art0349">
        <head>NEW G.M.</head>
        <p>THE NEW G.M.</p>
        <p>Bro. John Lavin, V.G.M„ is nominated for Grand Master.</p>
        <p>There are six nominations for the honour of Vice Grand Master, who graduates for Grand Master next year. These are Bros. Garlick, P.D.G.M., Macclesfield; Chas. Happs. Rotherham; G. H. Mather, P.D.G.M., Hull; Harry Perkins, P.D.G.M.. Leeds; T. H. Roberts, D.C.S.' Manchester and Salford; Edward Stan- ley, P.D.G.M., Newcastle-on-Tyne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0350" n="art0350">
        <head>SOCIAL PROGRAMME. The Mayor to Welcome Delegates to Warrington.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL PROGRAMME. The Mayor to Welcome Delegates to Warrington.</p>
        <p>The business of the Delegation will be trans- acted on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, but on Monday evening his Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) himself a prominent Friendly Society worker, and the Mayoress will hold a reception at the Town Hall of the Grand Officers and delegates and ladies accompanying them.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the delegates will drive into Cheshire, visit Alderman Bolton's orchid houses at Wilderspool, and return to head quarters, the Lion Hotel, for tea and entertainment. On Wednesday a complimentary banquet will be given to the delegates at the Lion Hotel, at which Mr. A. H. Crosfield will preside, sup- ported by the Grand Officers of the Order and other gentlemen. "The King," "Queen Mary," and "The Prince of Wales and Rest of the Royal Family" will be given from the chair; while Bro. A. H. Crosfield will also propose "The Order of Druids Friendly Society," to which Bros. R. Verney Clayton, G.T., and J. W. Shaw, G.S.. will respond. Other toasts will be: "Army and Navy," proposed by Bro. W. Bibby, responded to by Bro. A. Billington, P.G.M.; "The Town and Trade of Warrington," proposed by Bro. Alderman T. W. Grundy, P.G.M., and responded to by Alderman D. Tinnion ; "Warrington Equalised District," proposed by Bro. J. R.: Cap- ping, responded to by Bros. R. Perdue and Wm. Woods; "Kindred Societies, Hon. Members and Visitors," proposed 117 Bro. George Shaw, re- sponded to by Bro. T. P. Bradshaw and Alder- man Bolton; "The Grand Officers and Board ,of Management," proposed by Bro. George Blalk- burn, responded to by Bre. J. Lavin, V.G.M.;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0351" n="art0351">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Received a Commission.—Mr. Percy E. Eger- ton, of London-road, Stockton Heath, who recently gained his First Officer's Board of Trade certificate for the Mercantile Marine, has this week received a sub-lieutenant's commis- sion in the Royal Naval Reserve, and has re- ceived instruction to report at Devonport for active service.</p>
        <p>National Children's Home and Orphanage.- The house-to-house collection in the Stockton Heath district for the above institution resulted in the dispatch ,of the handsome sum of .25 10s. to the governor, an increase of 20s. over last year. The amounts collected were: Miss E. Brookes, 6s. id.; Miss Lily Berry, 3s. 9d.; Mrs. Clarke, 4s. 6W.; Mr. H. Ditchfield, 45.; Miss B. Fairhurst, 25.; Mrs. Gould, 7s. Old.; Miss S. Gamble, 6s. 10d.; Miss E. Houghton, 3s. 90.; Miss E. Hughes, 7s. 2d.; Miss Kirkland, 2s. 2d.; Miss Penketh, 4s. llfd.; Miss Powell, 4s. 3d.; Miss H. Turner, 2s. ld.•, Miss Turner, 58.; Misses Webb, 10s. 9W.; Mr. G. A. Whitfield, Bs.; Miss E. Whitfield, 3s. 4d.; Mrs. and Miss Williams, ss. 2d.; Mr. Wilcockson, 17s. Id.; small sums, is.. 110. Mr. E. A. R. Wilcockson is the local secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0352" n="art0352">
        <head>ELECTRIC LIGHT IN SIGHT.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT IN SIGHT.</p>
        <p>• The Beard of Trade have issued a Provisional Order under the provisions of the Electric Lighting Acts, with a view to the same being included with a Confirmation Bill about to be introduced into Parliament with a view to authorising the Warrington Corporation to supply electricity for public and private pur- poses within the parishes of Appleton, Grap- penhall, Latchford Without, Stockton Heath, Higher Walton, Lower Walton, and Thelwall, in the Rural District of Runcorn, in the County of Chester, and the Parishes or Townships of Penketh and Great Sankey. in the Rural Dis- trict of Warrington, in the County Palatine of Lancaster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0353" n="art0353">
        <head>KILLED WHILST WAVING TO COMRADE.</head>
        <p>KILLED WHILST WAVING TO COMRADE.</p>
        <p>Private Henry Taylor, of No. 1 Company of the National Reserve, 2nd/4th South Lancs. Regiment, was buried with full military honours in Stockton Heath Churchyard on Tuesday. Private Taylor was killed at Honey. Surrey, on Friday of last week, whilst engaged in guarding the London. Brighton and South Coast Railway Bridge there. He was knocked down by a slow train whilst returning the salute of a waving comrade in a passing express. Death was instantaneous. The funeral, which was attended by a large number of comrades-in-arms, from various regiments, under Lieutenants C. L. Williamson and Bur- gess, of the Warrington Territorials, was of a very impressive nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0354" n="art0354">
        <head>\ AIIINISTERED ALIEN.</head>
        <p>\ AIIINISTERED ALIEN.</p>
        <p>Case Against A Brazilian (ad Dismissed.</p>
        <p>Miss Pulseria Maria De Nacimento, Brazilian, was summoned at a special sitting of the Daresbury Petty Sess:onal Bench, c Thursday, for being an alien, and residing within a .prohibited area without registering herself, at Walton Inferior, on May 19th. Mr. George Wallington, of Walton Inferior, was summoned at the same time for failing to give notice of the presence of an alien in his house- hold.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Roberts and Mr. J. A. Ransome were on the Bench.</p>
        <p>Defendants pleaded guilty. A Prohibited Area.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Owen said that Walton Inferior was a prohibited area, ,and any alien staying within that area was expected to regis- ter. It was also the duty ç the person with whom the alien lodged to give notice to the police. The female defendant was born in Brazil, and was therefore an American sub- ject. She came to reside at Walton Inferior on May Bth, but it was not until the previous day (Wednesday), when' Inspector Breese met her at Stockton Heath, that the police knew of her presence in the neighbourhood. Inspector Breese said that at 12 noon on Wed- nesday he was in Victoria-avenue, Stockton Heath, when he met the female prisoner. Noticing the foreign appearance, he stopped her and asked her where she came from. She replied "America," and added that she was staying with Mrs. Wallington, an was looking after the baby. Witness made further inquiries and visited Mrs. Wallington, at 98, Ellesmere- road, where she was staying With her father. Witness then saw Mi. Wallington, who said that his daughter was on a visit from America. He admitted that the nurse was not registered, and said that he did not know it was required. He thought it was only Germans and Turks who had to register. Inspector Owen said that he was informed that the female prisoner had been .travelling in England for some little time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0355" n="art0355">
        <head>Matter of Inadvertence.</head>
        <p>A Matter of Inadvertence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington said that he did not know that there was any necessity to have the nurse registered. She had been with his daughter in England for some time. They had stayed at Blackpool, Matlock and Southport, and no question had ever been raised as to the neces- sity of the girl being registered. It was purely a matter of inadvertence. The girl did not understand the matter at all. He did not sup- pose she could read any notice that she might have seen about registration. The Chairman (Mr. H. Roberts) said that the Bench quite accepted Mr: Wallington's explanation. It was not one of the serious cases of the kind, or else the Bench would have exercised its full powers. The case against the girl would be dismissed, and Mr. Wallington would be called upon to pay the costs-ss. Mr. Wallington: Thank you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0356" n="art0356">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Temperance Meeting.—A. very interesting meeting was held in the Wesley Hall on Thurs- day evening, when the members of the Stockton Heath Band of Hope, under the direction of Mr. G. Whitfield, paid a visit to Padgate, and gave a series of songs, recitations, &amp;c., all bearing on the temperance question. A cantata represent- ing England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales, with Britannia on her throne, was very ably ren- dered by the Band of Hope members, the part of Britannia being taken by 'Miss Elsie Whitfield. Miss Eva Tanner sang with much feeling " England " and "There is a land," and gave "Merry month of May" as an encore. A duet by two little girls, Misses Daisy and Irene Summers, entitled "I don't want to play in your yard," created much amusement and re- ceived a well-deserved encore. The meeting closed with a humorous sketch entitled "Paddy's Pledge," which was well performed by the War- rington Temperance Guild Sketch Party.—A 14arty vote of thanks was proposed to all the artistes for the excellent programme they had rendereo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0357" n="art0357">
        <head>WELCOME DRUlDS!—Continued.</head>
        <p>WELCOME DRUlDS!—Continued.</p>
        <p>"The Chairman 2" proposed by Bro. J. Bibby ; and "The Press," proposed by Bro. A. J. Shaw.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS TO GRAND MASTER ALLEN.</p>
        <p>_ • Bro. Edwin Allen, G.M., will receive two presents, one by the local friendly societies, which will be handed to him by his Worship the Mayor, and the other by the Warrington District, for whom Bro. G. T. Rushton will officiate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0358" n="art0358">
        <head>LOCAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE LOCAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The local Friendly Societies' Committee who have had charge of the arrangements for the conference are :--r</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Forester). Bro. T. P. Bradshaw, P.P.G.M., Oddfellow (M.T.T.), chairman. ' Councillor John Chrimes, Chief Ranger, Foresters, vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>Bro. Robert Thoinley, District Secretary, Forestm, secretary. Bro. Edwin Allen, G.M., Druids. _ _</p>
        <p>The local secretary of the Druids committee is Bro. A. J. Shaw, 215, Tower View, Great Sankey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0359" n="art0359">
        <head>GUARDIANS' DILEMMA.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' DILEMMA.</p>
        <p>The Education Of The Cottage</p>
        <p>Homes' Children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0360" n="art0360">
        <head>"A REVOLUTIONARY PROPOSAL."</head>
        <p>"A REVOLUTIONARY PROPOSAL."</p>
        <p>A discussion took place at Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on the subject of the education of the children in the Cottage Homes. The problem hinged on the advisability or undesirability of having the children taught in the Council Schools instead of at the Homes. The principle of the Guardians surrendering part of their con- trol and the financial aspect were the chief points considered.</p>
        <p>DIRECTOR OF EDUCATION INTER- VIEWED.</p>
        <p>The controversy arose out of the following minute of the Cottage Homes Committee :7 " The Clerk reported that he had interviewed the Director of Education, Warrington, with regard to the amount the Guardians would have to pay if the children resident in the Cottage Homes were sent to the Council School, Oak- wood-avenue, for educational purposes. The Director had informed him that if there were, say, a hundred children in the Cottage Homes of school age, the amount the Guardians would have to pay would be about £l2O per annum. A return was presented showing the present number of children in the Homes to be as fol- lows: Between the ages of seven and fourteen, boys 33, girls 36; between fourteen and sixteen, boys 8, girls 12; under seven years of age, 27; total, 116. Of that number three are Roman Catholic children. It was decided, on the pro- position of Mr. Brooks, seconded by the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, that the Board be recom- mended to accept the terms of the Warrington Education Authority as they affected the chil- dren between the ages of seven and fourteen, and that as soon as expedient all the children of those ages be sent to the Council School, Oakwood-avenue, Warrington. It was moved by Mr. Brooks and seconded by the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, that the Local Government Board be asked to sanction the proposal of the Board to appoint Mrs. Lawrance superintendent and matron of the Cottage Homes, at a salary at the rate of £75 per annum, together with emolut meats, as heretofore. On a vote being taken, eight were in favour, none against, and one did not vote. In view of the Roman Catholic children having been removed from the Cot- tage Homes, there is now no further need for the services of the Roman Catholic instructor, and the committee recommended that this office be abolished, and that the Rev. Father Miller be acquainted accordingly." Mr. Mather asked why the matron had not been recommended for an increase of salary, along with the other officials. Mr. Brooks said that Mrs. Lawrance had not asked for an advance in salary.</p>
        <p>[The reader will recollect that on the death of Mr. Lawrance, the superintendent of the Homes, Mrs. Lawrance was re-appointed matron, with the additional title of superin- tendent. at a salary of £75 per annum. This appointment had to be endorsed by the Local Government Board before it became fully 'official.—Ed.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0361" n="art0361">
        <head>FOLLOWING OTHER UNIONS.</head>
        <p>FOLLOWING OTHER UNIONS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell stated that other Unions had done what the Guardians had done in the matter of Mrs. Lawrance. As regarded the question of salary, if they made Mrs. Lawrance's salary £lOO it wouldn't be too much. She was a splendid woman for the position, a fact that was reflected in the high terms in which H.M. Inspector's reports on his visit to the Homes were couched. • The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) remarked that the action of the Board in regard to Mrs. Lawrance was the only logical step following what they decided some time ago, viz., to send the children outside the Cottage Homes for educational purposes. It was a mistake to think it was going to b economical. But it was not merely a question. of finance; a matter of principle was involved. It meant that the Guardians were not going to carry out part of the duty which devolved upon them—the education of the children under their care. They would be carrying it out in a way, but it did not seem an altogether satisfactory way to leave the education of the children to persons over whom the Guardians had only slight and indirect control.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather said he thought that the fact that the majority of the children concerned were Warrington Children, made them quite eligible to attend the Warrington schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0362" n="art0362">
        <head>DRASTIC CHANGE.</head>
        <p>A DRASTIC CHANGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Billinge said it appeared to him that the whole thing was going to be a drastic change. He thought the members of the Board were not sufficiently informed to form a true opinion in the matter.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: :You will be later on. The whole thing is unusual. It's a proposal to revolutionise the whole system. Mr. Mather expressed the opinion that the members should be supplied by the Clerk with particulars of the whole matter Mr. Shaw indignantly declared that it was monstrous that an intelligent body of men should be treated in the way Mr. Mather had suggested the committee should be treated, as though they were not capable of dealing with the business themselves.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said it had been calculated that the change would cost the Guardians £230.</p>
        <p>The Chairman asked for .the source of the estimate.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell replied that it was calculated the previous day in committee. The Chairman _pointed out that a calculation made by a member or members of the com- mittee could not be accepted as official.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0363" n="art0363">
        <head>CATHOLIC CHILDREN ALREADY REMOVED.</head>
        <p>CATHOLIC CHILDREN ALREADY REMOVED.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent said that the Catholic chil- dren had already been sent from the Homes to the Catholic schools, and added that despite the lamented death of Mr. Lawrance, everything had worked admirably at the Homes; it was only fair to Mrs. Lawrance to remember that. It was quite natural that the Board should want' to have full information on the subject. The matter then dropped, the understanding being that the members should be supplied with the full facts of ,the matter with a view to sub- sequent discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0364" n="art0364">
        <head>"TALKING PICTURES" AT STAR</head>
        <p>"TALKING PICTURES" AT STAR</p>
        <p>KINEMA.</p>
        <p>At the Star Kinema this week, Warring- tonians have had the opportunity of witnessing performances that are unique locally—for their principal feature has been the kinetophone, that latest and most wonderful of. T. A. Edison's inventions. The kinetophone is the cinemato- graph and phonograph combined, with the result that the pictures " talk " in a wonderful way. There is no flaw in the workings; it is a distinctly new machine; and apparently there is no sound produced by human being or animal which cannot be truly reproduced on the kinetophone with ease. In short, Edison has revolutionised the "living picture" art. At each Kinema performance three separate kinetophonic films are shown.</p>
        <p>The -other items in the programmes are tip- top." On the first three days two powerful dramas, "The Miser's Daughter" and "The Veteran" were shown, catering capitally with the topical touch. Beautiful coloured pictures of scenery, and stories of child-life, were given amid loud applause. The striking filmed story of "The Lusitania " is also being shown— backed by a specially strong war selection. to-day's programmes include "Lord Kitchener in France "—a film which will be- come historic, and which should be missed on no account. It is shown by authority of the War Office.</p>
        <p>"4 Tillie's Punctured Romance" To Be Repeated. On the last three days of next week the big film which so recently packed the Star Kinema each time it was reeled off, viz., " Tillie's Punctured Romance," will be repeated. The leading line on the first three days will be "Beautiful Jim."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0365" n="art0365">
        <head>Death of Well-known Landlord.</head>
        <p>Death of Well-known Landlord.</p>
        <p>We regret to have to record that Mr. James Gleave, landlord of the Barley Mow, died early yesterday; morning after an operation.</p>
        <p>OU SU</p>
