      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0366" n="art0366">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER WAR-PLAY</p>
        <p>AT Tlig</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>- "Joy—Sister of Mercy," which is be 1,14 staged twice nightly at the Royal Court Them,/ this week, is a drama which—owing to t•'` war flavour about it and to the fact that it well-written and abounds in incidents-0011,1v not fail to be popular, and that it is poptuat with Warringtonians, has been attested by r.-u: large audiences. It is from the fruitful Pe,i' of Miss Eva Elwes and, from the rise to the fat! of the curtain, is calculated to grip the interesl of those whose taste runs to melodrama. It gets adequate representation from a capab_7 company sent on tour by Mr. T. Edward Miss Ward, in the title role, acts with powei; and is an immense favourite with he' audiences, and all the other parts al adequately sustained, there being plenty e, opportunities for telling work, of which fili; opportunity is taken, whilst the humour is jus"</p>
        <p>." SHEBA " NEXT WEEK. A Gorgeous Oriental Revue. I g' Oriental ,plendour, a capital story an( 0 sumptuous mounting are all aptly descriptis. of Dan Hardie's "Sheba," his latest vente in the domains of the popular modern rer4,..e An, original Mixture has resulted in an ePTA tainment which has taken every town by storm. This enterprising manager gathered around him a band of 30 talente artistes, second to none, for the worthy ir/.. terpretation of "Sheba." The bright and Per ticular star of the company is undoubtedi Katarina Renoir, who, unquestionably has very bright and successful future before All those competent to judge affirm that she ld a brilliant acquisition to the profession, en,. is destined to attain the very highest pinnae'Z of fame. Her marvellous toe dancing and e't tractive personality win favour even in the leaso likely • quarters, and she instantly becomes popular favourite wherever she apPeel Sheba" is described as an Egyptian musict!, comedy revue, and the comprehensive title We' expresses the entertainment. Gorgeous dreSsel magnificent scenery depicting the splendours 13. the Orient. and entrancing music are always evidence throughout the performance. The' will be a matinee on Whit Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-22_WidnesExaminer_art0367" n="art0367">
        <head>HAPPY HOURS AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>HAPPY HOURS AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There is an attractive series of turns at thje Hippodrome this week. Dorothy Haigh ant" Wm. Heilbron do well in the dramatic sketco; "The Straight Game," a thrilling episode life in North-West Canada. This effective little one-act play, which has some tense moments, iTEI admirably presented and meets with wefr merited appreciation. That well-known and 141" liable laugh manufacturer, Ernest Shatt?.,' amuses vastly with a smart . skit, and, with bz: charming little daughter, who sings deligbr fully, presents a coffee stall recruiting incident' Few children one has seen on the stage bev,„ff sung with the sweet, unharshened notes of OP' Miss Shand, whose claim to the title of favourite is undeniable, so far at least as concerns tb: particular performance we visited, when 110 young lady was enthusiastically applauded. 1:1,'„ Caron Troupe of eight French and Belgian Is gymnasts give an excellent account of the selves. A •musical item of deserved popularitY is the vocal scena, "The Desert," by Walt,el Barrett and - Ella Elgar, who both sing soli:. Delafo is a comedy juggler of great accomplisr; ment, who wins universal approval. Herber' La Martine executes evolutions in staircae° terpsichore with great dexterity, and Dess borough wins the good pleasure of the audiell'.: wtih popular songs. Mr. Harold March end merry men 'neath the footlights rend'" characteristic excellence an overture, " Br; Empire," and a selection from "The Boheulit"" Girl." The Gaumont Graphic completes sound and well-varied programme. Big Bill Next Week. A programme of all-round attractivenc_s Wig been arranged for next week at this ever4;l.; creasingly popular place of amusement. will be headed by Sid Cotterell and Co. in Substitute "—which is spoken of as an axce,-'.ll ingly strong show—and the bottom of the vlo turn will be supplied by Zella Vondi and Co. 1 a brilliant musical pot-pourri. Other atirc.O.,sB. five items will be given by Leon Bosco, a wildering musician; Tom Wild, in his lesque "The Quarrymen "; Sados, a novell'; juggler; Billie and Frank Bass, in comedy versation;_ the Edwards', high-class vocals"'</p>
        <p>A Present for Mr. Turpin. Next Friday evening a presentation will Pe. made at the Hippodrome to Mr. A. W. Tur01"0 the late and popular manager, who is conducting a large variety theatre in Soul' port.</p>
        <p>Life and Death. During the week ended May 15th, there wfs,o 32 births and 15 deaths in Warrington, excess of births over deaths being 17. In births there were two more males than fenial% and in the deaths three more males 0,93 females. The death-rate was 10.3 against It, in the corresponding week last year, and birth-rate 22.0 against 27.7. Three of the defil'" were from bronchitis. eSli During the week there were seventeen a- ni cases of scarlet fever, and the total nuixiber - cases of this disease in the hospital was 77*</p>
