        <p>nice. We are also well looked after. Fdo not know how long I shall be in this hospital, but I hope I shall be in England by the time you get this letter." • Joined Soon After the Outbreak. Private Howarth is a married man, and he resides with his wife at Long-row, Orford. He joined the Territorials on September Ist, and after undergoing his training, first in Scotland and then at Tunbridge Wells, he was drafted to the Front with the initial batch of men, on February 12th. Keen on Bowling. Private' Howarth was educated at Orford School. He was formerly employed at the Orford Tannery. He was very keen on bowl- ing, and had won several prizes. He wor- shipped at Orford Church, and was a member of the Young Men's Bible Class. He belongs to the Order of Oddfellows, and is a member of the Junior Conservative Club. Two of his brothers are with the Colours. One is.. in train- ing with the R.F.A., whilst another is serving at the Front in the same regiment and company as Private Howarth himself. A sten-brother—Corporal Higham, of Orford—was killed in action a short time ago. PRIVATE JOHN HENSHALL Hit In The Shoulder, But Doing Well. Last Saturday Private John Henshall, B Company, 4th South Lancashires, was wounded in the shoulder. He is a married man, and lives at 37, Bewsey-road. When the war broke out he was employed by Messrs. J. Cros- field and Sons in the glycerine department; and, being a Volunteer of seven years' standing, though he had been retired for four years, he rejoined and volunteered for active service. He was a member of the Liberal Club, and was well-known on the bowling green. Reported By Bosom Friend. Mrs. Henshall received the news of the casualty in the following letter, written by Private B. Watson, of the same company, and medical officer's servant :</p>
